window.OSAP_FT=window.OSAP_FT||{};window.OSAP_FT["20"]={"https://theconversation.com/do-youth-boot-camps-reduce-crime-what-the-evidence-says-about-tough-love-for-troubled-youths-296681":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-17T16:16Z","sha":"65d73ba314bf8eaea8c11675a0396160d15d312447fb44883fa6eb2a58539ce0","read":"https://theconversation.com/do-youth-boot-camps-reduce-crime-what-the-evidence-says-about-tough-love-for-troubled-youths-296681","bytes":179717,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Do youth boot camps reduce crime? What the evidence says about ‘tough love’ for troubled youths","by":[{"n":"Joel Robert McGregor","r":"Senior Lecturer, Criminology, Swinburne University of Technology"}],"pub":"2026-11-16T03:52:15Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["With ",["Victoria’s state election","https://theconversation.com/labor-slumps-to-new-lows-in-two-victorian-polls-296132"]," less than two weeks away, both major parties have proposed new ways to address youth crime and improve community safety."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more: ",["With two weeks to go, the Victorian election is the Coalition’s to lose","https://theconversation.com/with-two-weeks-to-go-the-victorian-election-is-the-coalitions-to-lose-296248"]]},{"k":"p","s":["Opposition Leader Jess Wilson has proposed introducing ",["youth justice boot camps","https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-11-15/dhurringile-liberals-jess-wilson-crime-bootcamp/107387262"],", while Labor Premier Ben Carroll has announced a ",["$204 million mandatory rehabilitation scheme","https://www.viclabor.org.au/news/tough-love-plan-to-detain-and-rehabilitate-youths"]]},{"k":"p","s":["What does the evidence tell us about whether these approaches actually work?"]},{"k":"h","s":["What are the parties proposing?"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Coalition proposes converting the former Dhurringile Prison, near Shepparton in northern Victoria, into a boot camp for offenders aged 14 to 17. The prison closed in 2024 due to a drop in demand for minimum-security beds."]},{"k":"p","s":["Under Wilson’s plan, courts could send young people there instead of imposing a community corrections order or another custodial sentence."]},{"k":"p","s":["Participants would follow strict daily routines involving farm work, cleaning, maintenance, cooking and education."]},{"k":"p","s":[["Wilson said","https://www.heraldsun.com.au/news/victoria/state-election/jess-wilson-promises-youth-crime-bootcamps-under-coalition-election-plan-to-cut-crime/news-story/aec6c743d5a7570fb97233456686bc76"]," the program would instil discipline and respect, teach practical skills and give young offenders a second chance."]},{"k":"p","s":["At our bootcamp there will be no soft options, no sitting around and no getting out of the hard work."]},{"k":"p","s":["Under Labor’s proposed “ ",["mandatory therapeutic orders","https://www.sbs.com.au/nitv/article/critics-slam-victorian-plan-for-mandatory-youth-rehabilitation/jfcru92tj"],"”, young people convicted of lower and mid-level offences (including theft, property damage and minor assaults) could be detained in secure residential facilities for up to 12 months."]},{"k":"p","s":["They would be required to participate in education, drug and alcohol treatment, mental health support and practical life skills programs."]},{"k":"p","s":["Carroll describes it as “tough love”, arguing rehabilitation should not be optional."]},{"k":"p","s":["Both parties argue their plans will help prevent further offending and make communities safer. But they take different approaches. Wilson’s proposal focuses on discipline and responsibility, while Carroll’s places greater emphasis on therapeutic rehabilitation."]},{"k":"p","s":["Despite these differences, both proposals involve removing young people from their communities and placing them in residential facilities. This includes some who might otherwise receive community-based sentences."]},{"k":"p","s":["This comes with risks. Bringing young offenders together can reinforce offending behaviour, while removing them from their families, schools and communities can disrupt the relationships and support that can help them stay out of trouble."]},{"k":"h","s":["A recent test case in Queensland"]},{"k":"p","s":["Queensland trialled youth justice boot camps ",["between 2013 and 2015","https://documents.parliament.qld.gov.au/com/LACSC-4B8C/C20152016-8140/tp1-20Aug2015.pdf"],". In the program, young people participated in structured daily activities, physical challenges, education and behavioural development. They were expected to follow routines and rules, with the intention of developing discipline, responsibility and skills to support their return to the community."]},{"k":"p","s":["However, an ",["independent evaluation","https://www.publications.qld.gov.au/dataset/final-report-for-the-evaluation-of-queenslands-youth-boot-camps/resource/a654be83-cd7f-43b3-b6cd-cdf2c9c8b48d"]," by KPMG identified significant problems with the program design, implementation and costs."]},{"k":"p","s":["The evaluation found some young people and their families reported improvements in confidence, behaviour and the ability to follow routines, but these benefits did not necessarily translate into reduced offending."]},{"k":"p","s":["Of the 35 young people who completed the program, 24 went on to re-offend. Re-offending was highest among those who did not receive support to reintegrate into their communities."]},{"k":"p","s":["This highlights the importance of helping young people reconnect with their families and communities, rather than simply focusing on what happens during their time in a residential program."]},{"k":"p","s":["Queensland subsequently ",["discontinued the boot camps","https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2015/aug/20/queensland-drops-expensive-failure-of-boot-camps-for-young-people"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["In September this year, Queensland ",["introduced its “Circuit Breaker”","https://statements.qld.gov.au/statements/106078"]," sentencing initiative. This program combines structured residential programs with rehabilitation and practical activities. Victoria’s Coalition has identified it as a model for its boot camps."]},{"k":"p","s":["However, Queensland’s new program is framed around therapeutic rehabilitation. Wilson’s emphasis on discipline, routine and personal responsibility suggests a greater focus on the traditional boot camp approach."]},{"k":"p","s":["In any case, it is too early to determine whether the new Queensland approach will reduce re-offending."]},{"k":"h","s":["So what actually works?"]},{"k":"p","s":["Young people don’t necessarily need to be placed in these types of programs to get the support they need to stop offending."]},{"k":"p","s":["In fact, the research shows that removing young people from their families, schools and communities can disrupt the very relationships and protective factors ",["that support their rehabilitation","https://doi.org/10.3390/socsci12090474"],". Community-based interventions can address offending behaviour, while allowing young people to maintain these important connections."]},{"k":"p","s":["Traditional boot camps emphasise discipline, physical training and strict behavioural control. While some programs have improved participants’ attitudes, physical fitness or behaviour, these improvements ",["have not consistently translated","https://doi.org/10.4073/csr.2005.6"]," into reduced offending after release."]},{"k":"p","s":["Therapeutic programs offer more hope. These focus on addressing the underlying factors that contribute to offending. The programs can include cognitive behavioural therapy, family-based interventions, and substance use, mental health and education support."]},{"k":"p","s":["Research shows these approaches ",["can reduce re-offending","https://doi.org/10.52922/ti04701"],". However, their effectiveness depends on program design, appropriate targeting and implementation."]},{"k":"h","s":["Solutions or just tough talk?"]},{"k":"p","s":["While both major parties are promising to make Victoria safer, we should be asking whether the proposals:"]},{"k":"li","s":["provide the right support"]},{"k":"li","s":["address the factors contributing to offending"]},{"k":"li","s":["help young people successfully return to their communities"]},{"k":"li","s":["are supported by evidence."]},{"k":"p","s":["If the answers to the above aren’t “yes”, then they’re likely just more cases of politicians talking tough on crime, with little evidence to lean on."]}],"chars":5701},"https://theconversation.com/burundis-refugees-are-under-pressure-to-return-why-some-choose-silence-when-they-do-293225":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-17T16:16Z","sha":"0ad378332e27fbe7e9fa8abd2062f30c83e91f7d7deb942a3599a3028b509322","read":"https://theconversation.com/burundis-refugees-are-under-pressure-to-return-why-some-choose-silence-when-they-do-293225","bytes":181055,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Burundi’s refugees are under pressure to return: why some choose silence when they do","by":[{"n":"Nina Soudan","r":"PhD researcher, Centre for Research on Peace and Development, KU Leuven"}],"pub":"2026-11-17T14:49:06Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["Burundi has experienced ",["repeated cycles of political violence and displacement","https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/politics-and-violence-in-burundi/9F1759A91BF39C59C98162C47E82C45A"],", notably following the ",["assassination of President Melchior Ndadayé","https://www.scirp.org/reference/referencespapers?referenceid=3391945"]," in 1993. Further unrest followed during an election-related political crisis in 2015."]},{"k":"p","s":["These cycles of unrest have resulted in thousands of deaths and forced hundreds of thousands of Burundians to flee their country. Over the past two decades, ",["more than 1.1 million","https://www.jointdatacenter.org/literature_review/home-again-refugee-return-and-post-conflict-violence-in-burundi/"]," have sought refuge in other countries, primarily in neighbouring Tanzania. Others have fled to Rwanda, the Democratic Republic of Congo, Uganda and further."]},{"k":"p","s":["There’s growing pressure from the Burundian state, as well as from neighbouring host countries, including ",["Tanzania","https://www.iwacu-burundi.org/rapatriement-volontaire-ou-refoulement-deguise/"],", ",["Uganda","https://www.sosmediasburundi.org/2026/08/05/apres-la-tanzanie-la-crainte-dune-nouvelle-vague-de-retours-forces-gagne-les-refugies-burundais-en-ouganda-et-en-zambie/"]," and ",["Kenya","https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c158wwl22xko"],", to encourage repatriation. This reflects a broader regional shift in which the continued presence of Burundian refugees outside their country is becoming increasingly conditional."]},{"k":"p","s":["Indeed, under international initiatives led by the UN refugee agency, Burundi has established a formal policy ",["framework","https://www.unhcr.org/news/press-releases/burundi-tanzania-unhcr-agree-tripartite-mechanism"]," for repatriation. But return to contexts of forced displacement, violence and other forms of persecution has prompted questions about whether it can work and how it is experienced by those who return."]},{"k":"p","s":["My doctoral research ",["examines the governance of return migration","https://soc.kuleuven.be/crpd/staff-researchers-and-visiting-fellows/00170376"],", specifically its long-term implications for returnees. In a ",["recent article","https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/17531055.2026.2711503"],", I explore the experience of Burundian returnees, drawing on 23 interviews with returnees, focus group discussions, and participant observation conducted in Burundi in 2024 and 2025."]},{"k":"p","s":["My research shows that return to “post-crisis” Burundi is much more than a physical or administrative process. It is a politicised action within the country’s history of conflicts and repeated displacement. I found that returnees were typically evasive about life in exile and chose their words carefully, so as not to give away too much about themselves."]},{"k":"p","s":["Their silence proposes, in its own way, an alternative account of return. It is an account that unsettles official narratives of “successful” return, reintegration and stability, and insists on the unresolved tensions that continue to structure life in Burundi."]},{"k":"h","s":["Return by all means"]},{"k":"p","s":["Voluntary repatriation has been on Burundi’s policy agenda for decades. Burundi and Tanzania first ",["agreed","https://www.unhcr.org/news/press-releases/burundi-tanzania-unhcr-agree-tripartite-mechanism"]," to a tripartite mechanism for refugee repatriation in 1998, with the support of the UN. The agreement has since been reactivated, most recently ",["in December 2025","https://www.unhcr.org/africa/news/press-releases/unhcr-governments-burundi-united-republic-tanzania-accelerate-returns-burundian"],". Between January and August 2026 alone, ",["96,602 refugees returned from Tanzania","https://data.unhcr.org/en/documents/details/124243"],", and ",["Nduta and Nyarugusu refugee camps","https://www.sosmediasburundi.org/en/2026/05/01/tanzania-nduta-camp-permanently-closed/"]," were progressively closed."]},{"k":"p","s":["Officials on both sides have portrayed this “acceleration” of return as a success story. Burundian authorities have repeatedly framed returns as ",["evidence of restored stability","https://news.un.org/en/story/2021/09/1101002"],", constructing the country as a “ ",["safe zone","https://academic.oup.com/jrs/article/34/3/2804/6104112"],"” to reinforce its political legitimacy."]},{"k":"p","s":["In practice, however, return has been shaped less by voluntariness than by a combination of incentives and constraints. In Tanzania, authorities have taken ",["measures to deter new arrivals and encourage refugee returns","https://www.migrationpolicy.org/journal/feature/tanzanias-open-door-refugees-narrows"],". They have reduced humanitarian aid, limited access to healthcare and education, and carried out ",["forced repatriation","https://www.iwacu-burundi.org/rapatriement-volontaire-ou-refoulement-deguise/"],". This ",["questions the character of these “voluntary” returns","https://www.thenewhumanitarian.org/opinion/2026/02/04/why-tanzania-mass-repatriation-burundi-refugees-must-be-stopped"],", and highlights their ",["coercive nature","https://issafrica.org/iss-today/how-voluntary-and-safe-are-burundian-refugee-returns-from-tanzania"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Several factors complicate the prospect of return as a lasting solution, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["a ",["severe economic crisis","https://afriquexxi.info/In-Burundi-a-deeply-desperate-population-and-a-regime-on-a-knife-edge"]]},{"k":"li","s":["limited access to employment, housing and essential services"]},{"k":"li","s":["concerns about ",["deteriorating human rights","https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2026/country-chapters/burundi"]]},{"k":"li","s":["the possibility of ",["renewed displacement","https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0962629822002098?via%3Dihub"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Return under a political cloud"]},{"k":"p","s":["My paper draws on field research in two sites: the borderland province of Muyinga, one of Burundi’s largest return destinations; and Bujumbura Mairie, the capital."]},{"k":"p","s":["Interviews reveal that return has become more politically charged, particularly since the 2015 political crisis, which triggered ",["renewed displacement","https://www.unhcr.org/news/briefing-notes/burundi-risks-becoming-forgotten-refugee-crisis-without-support"]," and intensified political repression. Returnees affiliated with opposition movements or particular ethnic factions may attract suspicion or surveillance and face ",["fears for their safety","https://www.globalr2p.org/publications/burundi-the-human-rights-council-should-further-extend-the-special-rapporteurs-mandate/"],", depending on where and when they were exiled."]},{"k":"p","s":["My research shows how silence provides space to navigate life upon return."]},{"k":"p","s":["Some returnees were evasive, carefully managing who to tell about their displacement, and who not to tell. One returnee confided his 2015 flight to a Tutsi friend while in exile, but swore him to secrecy, while avoiding the subject entirely with fellow Hutus. He felt they might ask why he left “when we stayed to defend our country”."]},{"k":"p","s":["For some, perceptions were informed less by who they were than by where they had been. They recalled that those who stayed behind could never be sure whether returnees had come back as “the Burundians they had once known”, or had returned changed, implicated in rebellion or sent back to destabilise the country."]},{"k":"p","s":["Others described forms of concealment centred on identity. One second-generation returnee adjusted her own accent so that she wouldn’t sound like an outsider."]},{"k":"p","s":["These forms of silence are about navigating political uncertainty and negotiating a place within an evolving social and political order."]},{"k":"p","s":["Silence, in this sense, can itself be a form of political agency. In selecting what aspects of their experience to disclose, to whom, and in what context, returnees are not simply protecting themselves. They are shaping the terms on which their experience enters public life at all."]},{"k":"h","s":["Understanding post-return"]},{"k":"p","s":["The experiences of Burundian returnees show that silence is not simply an absence of speech, but an active practice through which experiences of displacement are continuously negotiated."]},{"k":"p","s":["As more refugees are encouraged, pressured or compelled to return, understanding these post-return experiences becomes ever more important to help inform more realistic and responsive approaches to return."]},{"k":"p","s":["It matters for understanding the social and political conditions returnees encounter, but also the decisions they continue to face – whether to stay or to move again."]}],"chars":5957},"https://theconversation.com/could-your-personality-be-affecting-how-you-feel-about-social-media-296193":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-17T16:38Z","sha":"ae05a3f21a9f6b8d9e8cdd1a179f36e447fa56efa9587b346303eab673a8a54d","read":"https://theconversation.com/could-your-personality-be-affecting-how-you-feel-about-social-media-296193","bytes":180622,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Could your personality be affecting how you feel about social media?","by":[{"n":"Claire Hart","r":"Associate Professor of Psychology, University of Southampton"},{"n":"Carmen Șurariu","r":"PhD Candidate in Psychology, University of Southampton"},{"n":"Kathy Carnelley","r":"Associate Professor of Psychology, University of Southampton"}],"pub":"2026-11-16T16:35:50Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["Social media offers everyone the same basic promise: post something about yourself and get responses from other people. Whether it’s a holiday, a promotion or just a midweek meal, these days many people’s lives are shared online."]},{"k":"p","s":["But could it be that what users feel they get back from ",["the exchange","https://theconversation.com/topics/social-media-109"]," depends on their personality? ",["Our research","https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/0144929X.2026.2732026"]," has found that people who are higher in a trait called “narcissistic admiration” tend to be more positive about their online interactions. Narcissistic admiration is when someone goes out of their way to attract attention and validation as a way of boosting their self-esteem."]},{"k":"p","s":["At the same time, those higher in narcissistic rivalry (positioning themselves as superior to others) and narcissistic vulnerability (those struggling with insecurity) reported fewer favourable social outcomes."]},{"k":"p","s":["We initially thought differences in how they presented themselves online might help to explain these differences. But when we followed people’s actual social media posts over three weeks, we found that wasn’t the case. Instead, our findings pointed towards another possibility. What people expect from social media, and whether they feel they get it, may be an important part of the story."]},{"k":"p","s":["We often use the term “narcissistic” to describe someone who loves attention. But narcissistic traits exist on a continuum – we all have them to some degree. Psychologists also distinguish between ",["narcissistic traits","https://theconversation.com/what-weve-learned-about-narcissism-over-the-past-30-years-258505"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Narcissistic admiration involves wanting to stand out, impress others and gain admiration and status. Narcissistic rivalry is more antagonistic and involves maintaining superiority, partly by devaluing others."]},{"k":"p","s":["Vulnerable narcissism looks different. People higher in this trait tend to have a more fragile sense of self and greater sensitivity to rejection and how they feel they are being judged by their peers."]},{"k":"h","s":["What people really think of us"]},{"k":"p","s":["Social media is an interesting environment in which to study these traits. It offers opportunities to attract attention and admiration, but also frequent feedback about whether other people have responded as we hoped."]},{"k":"p","s":["In our research, we examined more than 1,000 social media users across Brazil, Indonesia and the UK. Participants completed questionnaires measuring their levels of narcissistic admiration, rivalry and vulnerability, alongside questions about what they felt they gained from their social media interactions."]},{"k":"p","s":["People higher in narcissistic admiration tended to feel they gained more from their social media interactions. This included factors such as greater levels of attention and validation, and more opportunities to initiate relationships."]},{"k":"p","s":["Those higher in narcissistic rivalry and vulnerability reported less favourable experiences. They felt less close to others and that they received less attention. This raised an obvious question: why?"]},{"k":"p","s":["One possibility was that people with different narcissistic traits were simply presenting themselves differently online. So, in ",["another paper","https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0092656626000346?via%3Dihub"]," we looked much more closely at what happened in people’s everyday social media lives."]},{"k":"p","s":["We followed 154 adults over 21 days, repeatedly asking them about posts they had made. Participants told us what they expected to get from their posts and, later, what they felt they had received. We also asked about their wellbeing."]},{"k":"p","s":["Some of the patterns we’d found previously appeared again. People higher in narcissistic admiration reported more favourable daily interpersonal outcomes. Those higher in rivalry reported poorer outcomes when it came to relationships – that is, initiating them, maintaining them and staying close to people. Vulnerable narcissism was associated with lower levels of closeness and less ability to maintain relationships."]},{"k":"p","s":["But differences in how people presented themselves online didn’t explain this variation in perceived social outcomes. Instead, people’s expectations provided an intriguing clue."]},{"k":"p","s":["People higher in vulnerable narcissism expected to be more popular and get more attention and validation from their social media posts than they subsequently felt they received."]},{"k":"p","s":["Vulnerable narcissism was also consistently associated with lower wellbeing in this study. And when people expected a post to help manage their relationships but then felt that it hadn’t, this mismatch was more keenly felt among those with higher levels of vulnerable narcissism."]},{"k":"p","s":["This doesn’t mean that unmet expectations caused poorer wellbeing. Nor did we objectively count every like, comment or message that our participants received. Our findings concern people’s perceptions of what they got back."]},{"k":"p","s":["But it suggests that understanding people’s experiences of social media may require looking beyond what they post. Who we are and what we expect from other people may matter too."]},{"k":"p","s":["So what can we do with this? Our findings don’t suggest that people should simply expect less from social media. Wanting connection, attention or reassurance from other people is a normal part of life."]},{"k":"p","s":["But they do suggest that it may be useful to think about what we are expecting a post to achieve. Are we sharing something because we want to connect with others? Are we looking for reassurance or validation? Are we hoping that one particular person will respond?"]},{"k":"p","s":["If the response feels disappointing, it may be worth considering not only what came back, but also what we had hoped would happen. Becoming more aware of those expectations may help us to understand our own reactions to social media. Otherwise, we could assume that how we feel is simply a consequence of the response itself."]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a broader lesson here too. Discussions about whether social media is “good” or “bad” for our wellbeing often focus on platforms, screen time or what people post. Our findings add another layer: the same online environment may be experienced very differently depending on the person using it and what they hope to get from it."]}],"chars":5867},"https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/guinea-bissau":{"r":"govuk","at":"2026-11-17T16:38Z","sha":"17bd4542f7115e9a38a5fd39be27384d529f09b8a1656695fa9230dffbbbdb30","read":"https://www.gov.uk/api/content/foreign-travel-advice/guinea-bissau","bytes":40043,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Guinea-Bissau travel advice","by":[{"n":"Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office","r":""}],"pub":"2013-01-07T00:00:00+00:00","mod":"2025-12-10T13:55:45+00:00","blocks":[{"k":"h","s":["Warnings and insurance"]},{"k":"h","s":["Before you travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["No travel can be guaranteed safe. Read all the advice in this guide. You may also find it helpful to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for women travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/advice-for-women-travelling-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read our ",["guide on disability and travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/disabled-travellers"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read about ",["safety for solo and independent travel","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/solo-and-independent-travel"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["advice on volunteering and adventure travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/safer-adventure-travel-and-volunteering-overseas"]]},{"k":"hs","s":["Travel insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you choose to travel, research your destinations and ",["get appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"],". Insurance should cover your itinerary, planned activities and expenses in an emergency."]},{"k":"h","s":["About FCDO travel advice"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) provides advice about risks of travel to help you make informed decisions. Find out ",["more about FCDO travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/guinea-bissau/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["Travel Aware on Instagram","https://www.instagram.com/travelaware/"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Entry requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["This information is for people travelling on a full ",["‘British citizen’ passport","https://www.gov.uk/types-of-british-nationality"]," from the UK. It is based on the UK government’s understanding of Guinea-Bissau’s current rules for the most common types of travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["The authorities in Guinea-Bissau set and enforce entry rules. If you’re not sure how these requirements apply to you, contact the Guinea-Bissau Embassy in Paris, France:"]},{"k":"p","s":["94 Rue St Lazare, 75009 Paris Email: embgbfr@gmail.com Telephone: +33 (0)1 48 74 36 39 or +33 (0)1 48 78 36 69"]},{"k":"h","s":["Passport validity requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["To enter Guinea-Bissau, your passport must have an ‘expiry date’ at least 6 months after the date you arrive."]},{"k":"p","s":["Check with your travel provider that your passport and other travel documents meet requirements. ",["Renew your passport","https://www.gov.uk/renew-adult-passport/renew"]," if you need to."]},{"k":"p","s":["You will be denied entry if you do not have a valid travel document or try to use a passport that has been reported lost or stolen."]},{"k":"h","s":["Dual nationals"]},{"k":"p","s":["When returning to the UK, British citizens must carry a valid ",["British passport","https://www.gov.uk/browse/abroad/passports"]," or ",["certificate of entitlement","https://www.gov.uk/right-of-abode/apply-for-a-certificate-of-entitlement"],". See ",["Dual citizenship","https://www.gov.uk/dual-citizenship"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Visa requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["You must have a visa to visit Guinea-Bissau."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Applying for a visa"]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact the Guinea-Bissau Embassy in Paris, France, for information on applying for a visa:"]},{"k":"p","s":["94 Rue St Lazare, 75009 Paris Email: embgbfr@gmail.com Telephone: +33 (0)1 48 74 36 39 or +33 (0)1 48 78 36 69"]},{"k":"p","s":["Guinea-Bissau also has embassies in neighbouring countries, including Senegal, where you may be able to get a visa."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["To enter Guinea-Bissau, you must have a certificate to prove you’ve had a yellow fever vaccination."]},{"k":"p","s":["For more details about medical entry requirements and recommended vaccinations, see ",["TravelHealthPro’s Guinea-Bissau guide","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/98/guinea-bissau#Vaccine_Recommendations"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Customs rules"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are strict rules about goods you can take into or out of Guinea-Bissau. You must declare anything that may be prohibited or subject to tax or duty."]},{"k":"h","s":["Safety and security"]},{"k":"h","s":["Terrorism"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a high threat of terrorist attack globally affecting UK interests and British nationals, including from groups and individuals who view the UK and British nationals as targets. Stay aware of your surroundings at all times."]},{"k":"p","s":["UK Counter Terrorism Policing has information and advice on ",["staying safe","https://www.counterterrorism.police.uk/runhidetell/"]," and what to do in the event of a terrorist attack. Find out ",["how to reduce your risk from terrorism while abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/reduce-your-risk-from-terrorism-while-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Terrorism in Guinea-Bissau"]},{"k":"p","s":["Although there is no recent history of terrorism in Guinea-Bissau, attacks cannot be ruled out."]},{"k":"p","s":["Attacks could be indiscriminate, including in places frequented by foreign nationals. Stay aware of your surroundings, keep up to date with local media reports and follow the advice of local authorities."]},{"k":"h","s":["Political situation"]},{"k":"p","s":["Guinea-Bissau suffers from political instability. In the event of any political unrest follow the advice of local authorities and monitor local media for information. Stay alert to local developments and avoid sensitive areas like military installations and government buildings. Avoid demonstrations or large public gatherings."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you have any doubts about the level of security in an area, stay at home or in your hotel."]},{"k":"h","s":["Crime"]},{"k":"p","s":["Pickpocketing and bag and phone snatching are relatively common in the capital city, Bissau. Where possible, keep valuables such as phones hidden when out in public."]},{"k":"p","s":["Be especially careful in remote areas where security forces have a limited presence."]},{"k":"h","s":["Landmines"]},{"k":"p","s":["Landmines remain a risk in parts of the country, and demining operations continue. The national demining centre (CAAMI) declared Bissau free from landmines. Outside of Bissau, take local advice and stay on paved roads."]},{"k":"h","s":["Laws and cultural differences"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Personal ID"]},{"k":"p","s":["You must carry ID such as a passport or residence permit, particularly when driving or in a taxi. Police forces regularly ask for ID."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Illegal drugs and prison sentences"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are severe penalties for all drug offences. Local prison conditions are harsh."]},{"k":"hs","s":["LGBT+ travellers"]},{"k":"p","s":["Same-sex sexual relationships are not criminalised. However, Guinea-Bissau is a conservative society and same-sex couples showing affection in public may attract unwanted and negative attention."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more ",["advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Money"]},{"k":"p","s":["Guinea-Bissau is a cash-based economy. Credit cards are rarely used and there are not many ATMs in the country. If you need to exchange foreign currency, use an official bureau de change."]},{"k":"h","s":["Transport risks"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are planning to drive in Guinea-Bissau, see ",["information on driving abroad","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["You can use a UK photocard driving licence to drive in Guinea-Bissau for up to 3 months. If you still have a paper driving licence, you may need to ",["update it to a photocard licence","https://www.gov.uk/exchange-paper-driving-licence"]," or get the ",["correct version of the international driving permit (IDP)","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad/international-driving-permit"]," as well. You’ll also need a registration card number with you in the vehicle – your car hire firm should provide this."]},{"k":"p","s":["It’s recommended you get local car insurance recognised by the ",["Economic Community of West African States (ECOWAS) Brown Card scheme","https://www.browncard.org/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Hire car companies often have stricter requirements for their customers, such as a year of driving experience, a higher minimum age and holding an IDP."]},{"k":"p","s":["Drink-driving is a serious offence in Guinea-Bissau. If you are tested and found to have any alcohol in your system, you may get a fine and a possible prison sentence."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road conditions"]},{"k":"p","s":["Traffic is generally light but driving standards and road conditions, including in Bissau, are poor. Avoid road travel at night and take suitable precautions in the rainy season, which runs from June to October. Heavy rains can make road and driving conditions difficult."]},{"k":"h","s":["Health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Before you travel check that:"]},{"k":"li","s":["your destination can provide the healthcare you may need"]},{"k":"li","s":["you have ",["appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"]," for local treatment or unexpected medical evacuation"]},{"k":"p","s":["This is particularly important if you have a health condition or are pregnant."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency medical number"]},{"k":"p","s":["Call 1313 and ask for an ambulance."]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your insurance company quickly if you’re referred to a medical facility for treatment."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine recommendations and health risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["At least 8 weeks before your trip:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check the latest ",["vaccine recommendations for Guinea-Bissau","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/98/guinea-bissau#Vaccine_Recommendations"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see where to get vaccines and whether you have to pay on the ",["NHS travel vaccinations page","https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/travel-vaccinations/"]]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["what health risks you’ll face in Guinea-Bissau","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/98/guinea-bissau"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["dengue"]},{"k":"li","s":["Zika virus"]},{"k":"li","s":["yellow fever"]},{"k":"li","s":["schistosomiasis"]},{"k":"li","s":["malaria"]},{"k":"hs","s":["HIV/AIDS"]},{"k":"p","s":["The United Nations International Children’s Emergency Fund’s report for 2022 estimated that 34,000 people (adults and children) ",["in Guinea-Bissau were living with HIV","https://www.unaids.org/en/regionscountries/countries/guinea-bissau"],". The prevalence percentage was estimated at around 2.4% of the adult population compared to the prevalence percentage in adults in the UK of around 0.2%. You should use normal precautions to avoid exposure to HIV/AIDS."]},{"k":"h","s":["Medication"]},{"k":"p","s":["Pharmacies in Guinea-Bissau are limited and may sometimes lack supplies. Pharmacies will accept UK prescriptions and are the only place you can buy medicines. Pharmacies in Guinea-Bissau do not have accreditation with UK insurance companies, so speak to your insurance company beforehand if you need a refund."]},{"k":"p","s":["The legal status and regulation of some medicines prescribed or bought in the UK can be different in other countries."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["best practice when travelling with medicines on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/43/medicines-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Healthcare in Guinea-Bissau"]},{"k":"p","s":["Medical facilities in Guinea-Bissau are extremely limited and hospitals are not fully operational. Make sure you have adequate travel health insurance and accessible funds to cover the cost of any medical treatment abroad and repatriation."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travel and mental health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["FCDO guidance on travel and mental health","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-advice-for-people-with-mental-health-issues"],". There is also ",["mental health guidance on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/85/travelling-with-mental-health-conditions"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Getting help"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) cannot provide tailored advice for individual trips. Read this travel advice and carry out your own research before deciding whether to travel."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency services in Guinea-Bissau"]},{"k":"p","s":["Ambulance: 1313"]},{"k":"p","s":["Fire: 1313"]},{"k":"p","s":["Police: 117"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contact your travel provider and insurer"]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your travel provider and your insurer if you are involved in a serious incident or emergency abroad. They will tell you if they can help and what you need to do."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Refunds and changes to travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["For refunds or changes to travel, contact your travel provider. You may also be able to make a claim through insurance. However, insurers usually require you to talk to your travel provider first."]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out more about ",["changing or cancelling travel plans","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice#changing-or-cancelling-travel-plans"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["where to get advice if you are in a dispute with a provider"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to access previous versions of travel advice to support a claim"]},{"k":"h","s":["Support from FCDO"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has guidance on staying safe and what to do if you need help or support abroad, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["being ",["arrested or imprisoned in Guinea-Bissau","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/guinea-bissau-prisoner-pack"]]},{"k":"li","s":["dealing with a ",["death abroad","https://www.gov.uk/after-a-death/death-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["getting help if you’re a ",["victim of crime","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/victim-of-crime-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["what to do if you’re ",["in hospital","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/in-hospital-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["if you’re ",["affected by a crisis","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/how-to-deal-with-a-crisis-overseas"],", such as a terrorist attack"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contacting FCDO"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help abroad"]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["how to get help from the UK government abroad","https://www.contact.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/posts/guinea-bissau/british-embassy-dakar"]," including in an emergency."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help in the UK"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can call FCDO in London if you need urgent help because something has happened to a friend or relative abroad."]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 020 7008 5000 (24 hours)"]},{"k":"p","s":[["Find out about call charges","https://www.gov.uk/call-charges"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/guinea-bissau/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]}],"chars":10114,"change":"New information about dual nationals returning to the UK (‘Entry requirements’ page)."},"https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/st-kitts-and-nevis":{"r":"govuk","at":"2026-11-17T17:03Z","sha":"225001faea97c2ae4a59b2ce1a3e5df0ed9b79ce0c6b861c9e871716db548f31","read":"https://www.gov.uk/api/content/foreign-travel-advice/st-kitts-and-nevis","bytes":52236,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"St Kitts and Nevis travel advice","by":[{"n":"Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office","r":""}],"pub":"2013-02-11T00:00:00+00:00","mod":"2026-01-05T14:54:43+00:00","blocks":[{"k":"h","s":["Warnings and insurance"]},{"k":"h","s":["Before you travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["No travel can be guaranteed safe. Read all the advice in this guide. You may also find it helpful to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for women travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/advice-for-women-travelling-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read our ",["guide on disability and travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/disabled-travellers"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read about ",["safety for solo and independent travel","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/solo-and-independent-travel"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["advice on volunteering and adventure travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/safer-adventure-travel-and-volunteering-overseas"]]},{"k":"hs","s":["Travel insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you choose to travel, research your destinations and ",["get appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"],". Insurance should cover your itinerary, planned activities and expenses in an emergency."]},{"k":"h","s":["About FCDO travel advice"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) provides advice about risks of travel to help you make informed decisions. Find out ",["more about FCDO travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/st-kitts-and-nevis/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["Travel Aware on Instagram","https://www.instagram.com/accounts/login/?next=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.instagram.com%2Ftravelaware%2F&is_from_rle"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Entry requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["This information is for people travelling on a full ",["‘British citizen’ passport","https://www.gov.uk/types-of-british-nationality"]," from the UK. It is based on the UK government’s understanding of St Kitts and Nevis’s current rules for the most common types of travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["The authorities in St Kitts and Nevis set and enforce entry rules. If you’re not sure how these requirements apply to you, contact the ",["St Kitts and Nevis High Commission in the UK","https://www.stkittsnevishcuk.gov.kn/"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Passport validity requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["To enter St Kitts and Nevis, your passport must have an ‘expiry date’ at least 6 months after the date you arrive."]},{"k":"p","s":["Check with your travel provider that your passport and other travel documents meet requirements. ",["Renew your passport","https://www.gov.uk/renew-adult-passport/renew"]," if you need to."]},{"k":"p","s":["You will be denied entry if you do not have a valid travel document or try to use a passport that has been reported lost or stolen."]},{"k":"h","s":["Dual nationals"]},{"k":"p","s":["When returning to the UK, British citizens must carry a valid ",["British passport","https://www.gov.uk/browse/abroad/passports"]," or ",["certificate of entitlement","https://www.gov.uk/right-of-abode/apply-for-a-certificate-of-entitlement"],". See ",["Dual citizenship","https://www.gov.uk/dual-citizenship"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Visa requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can visit St Kitts and Nevis without a visa for up to 6 months."]},{"k":"p","s":["All visitors to St Kitts and Nevis must obtain an Electronic Travel Authorisation (eTA) before travel. The eTA can be applied for online or via the official mobile app. For full details, see the ",["St Kitts and Nevis Electronic Border Management System website","https://www.knatravelform.kn/en"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you want to stay longer, you must apply and pay for an extension through the ",["St Kitts and Nevis Immigration Department","https://www.facebook.com/p/St-Kitts-Nevis-Immigration-Department-100064749728765/"],". Email: stkittsimmigrationdept@gmail.com."]},{"k":"p","s":["It is illegal to overstay your visa or to work without a work permit."]},{"k":"h","s":["Immigration form"]},{"k":"p","s":["Before you travel, ",["complete the immigration form online","https://www.knatravelform.kn/"]," to speed up your arrival in St Kitts and Nevis. You can also complete the form when you arrive using your personal device with a data connection or the free public wifi."]},{"k":"h","s":["Arriving by sea"]},{"k":"p","s":["You must contact the St Kitts and Nevis Port Authority at least 24 hours before arriving by sea. You must:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["fill in the immigration form","https://www.knatravelform.kn/"]]},{"k":"li","s":["contact either the ",["Nevis Air and Sea Ports Authority","https://www.nevisportsauthority.com/"],", for Nevis, or the ",["St Christopher Air and Sea Ports Authority","https://scaspa.com/"],", for St Kitts"]},{"k":"h","s":["Airport tax"]},{"k":"p","s":["Arrival and departure tax is usually included in the ticket price of most commercial flights."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re leaving from Robert L Bradshaw International Airport on a flight operated by LIAT, you must pay the departure tax cash or card at the Airport Finance Office."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["To enter St Kitts and Nevis, you must have a certificate to prove you’ve had a yellow fever vaccination if you’re coming from a ",["country listed as a transmission risk","https://nathnacyfzone.org.uk/factsheet/65/countries-with-risk-of-yellow-fever-transmission"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["For full details about medical entry requirements and recommended vaccinations, see ",["TravelHealthPro’s St Kitts and Nevis guide","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/207/st-kitts-and-nevis#Vaccine_Recommendations"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Customs rules"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are strict rules about ",["goods you can take into or out of St Kitts and Nevis","https://skncustoms.com/Show-Page.aspx?PageID=392"],". You must declare anything that may be prohibited or subject to tax or duty."]},{"k":"h","s":["Safety and security"]},{"k":"h","s":["Terrorism"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a high threat of terrorist attack globally affecting UK interests and British nationals, including from groups and individuals who view the UK and British nationals as targets. Stay aware of your surroundings at all times."]},{"k":"p","s":["UK Counter Terrorism Policing has information and advice on ",["staying safe","https://www.counterterrorism.police.uk/runhidetell/"]," and what to do in the event of a terrorist attack. Find out ",["how to reduce your risk from terrorism while abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/reduce-your-risk-from-terrorism-while-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Terrorism in St Kitts and Nevis"]},{"k":"p","s":["Although there is no recent history of terrorism in St Kitts and Nevis, attacks cannot be ruled out."]},{"k":"h","s":["Crime"]},{"k":"p","s":["There have been incidents of serious crime including murder, armed robbery and sexual assault."]},{"k":"p","s":["Take precautions to protect your personal safety:"]},{"k":"li","s":["make sure your accommodation is secure – this also applies if you’re staying on a yacht"]},{"k":"li","s":["take care when walking alone off main roads"]},{"k":"li","s":["take particular care around large gatherings, including street and beach parties, especially after dark or during the festival season"]},{"k":"li","s":["avoid isolated areas, including beaches, after dark"]},{"k":"li","s":["only use licensed taxis"]},{"k":"li","s":["do not carry large amounts of cash or jewellery"]},{"k":"li","s":["leave valuables and travel documents in your hotel safe or a safety deposit box"]},{"k":"li","s":["keep car doors locked when driving and do not stop if pedestrians try to flag down your vehicle"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Protecting your belongings"]},{"k":"p","s":["Do not carry large amounts of cash or wear expensive-looking jewellery or watches. Where possible leave valuables and travel documents in a safe deposit box or hotel safe."]},{"k":"h","s":["Laws and cultural differences"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Camouflage clothing"]},{"k":"p","s":["It is illegal for anyone, including children, to dress in camouflage clothing. You could get a 250 Eastern Caribbean dollar (EC dollar) fine and 3 months in prison."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Illegal drugs and prison sentences"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are severe penalties for all drug offences, particularly drug trafficking. If convicted at Magistrates Court, you could get a fine of up to 400,000 EC dollars or 3 times the street value, whichever is higher, and up to 10 years in prison. If the case goes to the High Court, sentences are a minimum of 15 years, up to life imprisonment."]},{"k":"p","s":["Marijuana (cannabis) for personal use has been decriminalised, but you must have a licence to use it. There are ",["fines for smoking of marijuana","https://cannabisclarityskn.com/"]," in some public places or without a licence."]},{"k":"hs","s":["LGBT+ travellers"]},{"k":"p","s":["Attitudes towards the LGBT+ community are mostly conservative in the Caribbean. Showing affection in public (for example, holding hands or kissing) is uncommon for opposite or same-sex couples and may receive unwanted and negative attention."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more ",["advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Outdoor activities and adventure tourism"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Swimming safety"]},{"k":"p","s":["Take care when swimming. Currents can be deceptively strong and not all beaches have lifeguards and warning flags. Monitor all beaches carefully and follow warnings."]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["water safety on holiday","https://www.rlss.org.uk/safety-on-holiday"]," from the Royal Life Saving Society."]},{"k":"h","s":["Transport risks"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are planning to drive in St Kitts and Nevis, see ",["information on driving abroad","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["You must have a St Kitts and Nevis driving licence. You can ",["buy a temporary licence from the Inland Revenue Department","https://www.sknird.com/drivers-licence"],", valid for either 3 months (62.50 EC dollars) or one year (125 EC dollars). If you’re hiring a vehicle, the hire company will usually help with this process. You will need to show your UK driving licence and passport."]},{"k":"p","s":["Hire car companies often have stricter requirements for their customers, such as a year of driving experience or a higher minimum age for driving. The minimum age to hire a vehicle is 21 years, but most car hire companies require you to be 25 or older."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you have an accident, phone the police on 911 and do not move your vehicle."]},{"k":"p","s":["Drink-driving is illegal in St Kitts and Nevis. If you are tested and found to have more than 80mg of alcohol in 100mg of blood the penalties are a fine of:"]},{"k":"li","s":["2,000 EC dollars and 12 months’ imprisonment for a first offence"]},{"k":"li","s":["4,000 EC dollars and 2 years’ imprisonment for subsequent offences"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Driving standards and road conditions"]},{"k":"p","s":["In St Kitts and Nevis:"]},{"k":"li","s":["there are potholes and unmarked speed bumps on minor roads"]},{"k":"li","s":["roads can be narrow with blind corners in rural areas"]},{"k":"li","s":["some roads are unlit at night"]},{"k":"li","s":["pedestrians often walk on the roads"]},{"k":"li","s":["drivers do not always use their indicators"]},{"k":"li","s":["road signs and hazards may not be easy to see"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Taxis"]},{"k":"p","s":["Only use licensed taxis. Licensed taxi operators carry or display identification, and you can identify the taxis by the yellow licence plate with ‘T’ or ‘TA’ in the number. Unlicensed taxis often do not have insurance to carry passengers."]},{"k":"p","s":["Taxis are not metered. There are standard taxi fares for most destinations. Agree the fare with the driver before you set off. You can often pay in US dollars as well as EC dollars."]},{"k":"h","s":["Extreme weather and natural disasters"]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out what you can do to prepare for and respond to ",["extreme weather and natural hazards","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/tropical-cyclones"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Hurricanes"]},{"k":"p","s":["The hurricane season runs from June to November."]},{"k":"p","s":["Monitor local and international weather updates from the ",["World Meteorological Organization","https://severeweather.wmo.int/"]," and the US National Hurricane Center, and follow the advice of ",["local authorities","https://www.sknis.gov.kn/"],", including any evacuation orders. See ",["Extreme weather and natural disasters","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/tropical-cyclones"]," for more information."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Wildfires"]},{"k":"p","s":["During especially hot and dry periods, a single spark or flame can start a wildfire. Report any fires to the emergency services immediately. If you start a wildfire (or attempt to start one), you can get up to 10 years in prison."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Earthquakes"]},{"k":"p","s":["Earthquakes are a risk in St Kitts and Nevis. The US Federal Emergency Management Agency website has advice about ",["what to do before, during and after an earthquake","https://www.ready.gov/earthquakes"],". If there is an earthquake, follow advice from the local authorities."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Volcanoes"]},{"k":"p","s":["Mount Liamuiga is a potentially active volcano and is likely to erupt in the future. The ",["University of West Indies (UWI) Seismic Research Centre","https://uwiseismic.com/island-profiles/st-kitts/"]," monitors volcanic activity on St Kitts and Nevis."]},{"k":"h","s":["Health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Before you travel check that:"]},{"k":"li","s":["your destination can provide the healthcare you may need"]},{"k":"li","s":["you have ",["appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"]," for local treatment or unexpected medical evacuation"]},{"k":"p","s":["This is particularly important if you have a health condition or are pregnant."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency medical number"]},{"k":"p","s":["Call 911 and ask for an ambulance."]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your insurance company quickly if you’re referred to a medical facility for treatment."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine recommendations and health risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["At least 8 weeks before your trip:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check the latest ",["vaccine recommendations for St Kitts and Nevis","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/207/st-kitts-and-nevis#Vaccine_Recommendations"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see where to get vaccines and whether you have to pay on the ",["NHS travel vaccinations page","https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/travel-vaccinations/"]]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["what health risks you’ll face in St Kitts and Nevis","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/207/st-kitts-and-nevis"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Medication"]},{"k":"p","s":["The legal status and regulation of some medicines prescribed or bought in the UK can be different in other countries."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["best practice when travelling with medicines on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/43/medicines-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Healthcare in St Kitts and Nevis"]},{"k":"p","s":["The main government hospital in St Kitts is the Joseph N France General Hospital in Brumaire. For Nevis the main government hospital is Alexandra Hospital in Charlestown. Both hospitals can provide many forms of treatment. However, serious cases may require emergency evacuation."]},{"k":"p","s":["Medical treatment in St Kitts and Nevis is expensive. If you choose to be treated at a private medical facility, check first if they accept medical travel insurance."]},{"k":"p","s":["Make sure you have adequate medical travel insurance and or accessible funds to cover the cost of medical treatment and repatriation."]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has a ",["list of medical providers in St Kitts and Nevis","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/st-kitts-nevis-list-of-medical-facilities-practioners"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travel and mental health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["FCDO guidance on travel and mental health","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-advice-for-people-with-mental-health-issues"],". There is also ",["mental health guidance on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/85/travelling-with-mental-health-conditions"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Getting help"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) cannot provide tailored advice for individual trips. Read this travel advice and carry out your own research before deciding whether to travel."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency services in St Kitts and Nevis"]},{"k":"p","s":["Ambulance: 911"]},{"k":"p","s":["Fire: 333"]},{"k":"p","s":["Police: 911"]},{"k":"p","s":["Coastguard: 465 8484 or 466 9280"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contact your travel provider and insurer"]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your travel provider and your insurer if you are involved in a serious incident or emergency abroad. They will tell you if they can help and what you need to do."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Refunds and changes to travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["For refunds or changes to travel, contact your travel provider. You may also be able to make a claim through insurance. However, insurers usually require you to talk to your travel provider first."]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out more about ",["changing or cancelling travel plans","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice#changing-or-cancelling-travel-plans"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["where to get advice if you are in a dispute with a provider"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to access previous versions of travel advice to support a claim"]},{"k":"h","s":["Support from FCDO"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has guidance on staying safe and what to do if you need help or support abroad, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["finding ",["lawyers","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/lawyers?country=saint%20kitts%20and%20nevis"]," and ",["funeral directors","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/funeral-directors?country=saint%20kitts%20and%20nevis"]," in St Kitts and Nevis"]},{"k":"li","s":["dealing with a ",["death in St Kitts and Nevis","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/when-someone-dies-in-st-kitts-and-nevis"]]},{"k":"li","s":["being ",["arrested or imprisoned in St Kitts and Nevis","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/st-kitts-and-nevis-prisoner-pack"]]},{"k":"li","s":["getting help if you’re a ",["victim of crime","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/victim-of-crime-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["what to do if you’re ",["in hospital","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/in-hospital-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["if you’re ",["affected by a crisis","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/how-to-deal-with-a-crisis-overseas"],", such as a terrorist attack"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contacting FCDO"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help abroad"]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["how to get help from the UK government abroad","https://www.contact.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/posts/st-kitts-and-nevis/british-high-commission-bridgetown"]," including in an emergency."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help in the UK"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can call FCDO in London if you need urgent help because something has happened to a friend or relative abroad."]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 020 7008 5000 (24 hours)"]},{"k":"p","s":[["Find out about call charges","https://www.gov.uk/call-charges"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/st-kitts-and-nevis/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]}],"chars":12980,"change":"Addition of information that all visitors to St Kitts and Nevis must obtain an Electronic Travel Authorisation before travel (‘Entry requirements’ page)."},"https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/malaysia":{"r":"govuk","at":"2026-11-20T17:04Z","sha":"55ced78740f60a264ed6fc90fc0c28a0f43acb0e7adcd4e23ed5ed044d5fa2f0","read":"https://www.gov.uk/api/content/foreign-travel-advice/malaysia","bytes":74117,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Malaysia travel advice","by":[{"n":"Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office","r":""}],"pub":"2013-03-11T00:00:00+00:00","mod":"2026-11-18T15:21:43+00:00","blocks":[{"k":"h","s":["Warnings and insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["Your travel insurance could be invalidated if you travel against advice from the Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO)."]},{"k":"h","s":["Areas where FCDO advises against all but essential travel"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Eastern Sabah coastal islands"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all but essential travel to all islands and dive sites off the coast of eastern Sabah from Sandakan to Tawau, including Lankayan Island, due to the threat of kidnapping. This does not apply to the mainland of Sabah."]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out more about ",["why FCDO advises against travel","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/malaysia/regional-risks"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Cannabis and drug offences"]},{"k":"p","s":["It is illegal to possess, use, traffic, import or export cannabis and other controlled drugs in Malaysia, including while transiting through to other countries. Many international airports use advance screening technology and may scan the baggage of transit passengers."]},{"k":"p","s":["Malaysia has some of the world’s strictest drug laws. Severe penalties apply, including long prison sentences of up to 20 years, caning, and the death penalty."]},{"k":"p","s":["See more information on the ",["Safety and security","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/malaysia/safety-and-security"]," page."]},{"k":"h","s":["Global travel impacts due to escalation in the Middle East"]},{"k":"p","s":["Escalation in the Middle East has caused widespread travel disruption, including airspace closures, delayed and cancelled flights."]},{"k":"p","s":["Your travel plans may be affected, even if your destination is not in the Middle East. Before you travel:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check travel advice for any countries or territories you are transiting through"]},{"k":"li","s":["check for the latest updates from your airline or tour operator"]},{"k":"li","s":["review your travel insurance policy for coverage"]},{"k":"li","s":["monitor local and international media for the latest information and sign up for travel advice email alerts."]},{"k":"li","s":["be mindful of your permitted length of stay in Malaysia"]},{"k":"h","s":["Before you travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["No travel can be guaranteed safe. Read all the advice in this guide. You may also find it helpful to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for women travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/advice-for-women-travelling-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read our ",["guide on disability and travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/disabled-travellers"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read about ",["safety for solo and independent travel","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/solo-and-independent-travel"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["advice on volunteering and adventure travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/safer-adventure-travel-and-volunteering-overseas"]]},{"k":"hs","s":["Travel insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you choose to travel, research your destinations and ",["get appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"],". Insurance should cover your itinerary, planned activities and expenses in an emergency."]},{"k":"h","s":["About FCDO travel advice"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO provides advice about risks of travel to help you make informed decisions. Find out ",["more about FCDO travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/malaysia/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["Travel Aware on Instagram","https://www.instagram.com/accounts/login/?next=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.instagram.com%2Ftravelaware%2F&is_from_rle"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Entry requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["This information is for people travelling on a full ",["‘British citizen’ passport","https://www.gov.uk/types-of-british-nationality"]," from the UK. It is based on the UK government’s understanding of Malaysia’s current rules for the most common types of travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["The authorities in Malaysia set and enforce entry rules. If you’re not sure how these requirements apply to you, contact the ",["Malaysian High Commission in the UK","https://www.kln.gov.my/web/gbr_london/"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Passport validity requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["To enter Malaysia, your passport must have an ‘expiry date’ at least 6 months after the date you arrive. Make sure it is undamaged, with no pages missing."]},{"k":"p","s":["Check with your travel provider that your passport and other travel documents meet requirements. ",["Renew your passport","https://www.gov.uk/renew-adult-passport/renew"]," if you need to."]},{"k":"p","s":["You will be denied entry if you do not have a valid travel document or try to use a passport that has been reported lost or stolen."]},{"k":"h","s":["Dual nationals"]},{"k":"p","s":["Malaysia does not recognise dual nationality. You can be refused entry if you’re found with a Malaysian passport and one of a different nationality. If you have dual nationality (not including Malaysian), enter and exit on the same passport."]},{"k":"p","s":["When returning to the UK, British citizens must carry a valid ",["British passport","https://www.gov.uk/browse/abroad/passports"]," or ",["certificate of entitlement","https://www.gov.uk/right-of-abode/apply-for-a-certificate-of-entitlement"],". See ",["Dual citizenship","https://www.gov.uk/dual-citizenship"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Visa requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can visit Malaysia without a visa for tourism. You will normally be allowed to stay for 90 days on arrival."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you want to stay for longer than 90 days or are not a tourist, you must apply for a visa from the nearest ",["Malaysian diplomatic mission","https://www.kln.gov.my/web/guest/overseas-mission"]," before you travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["The Malaysian authorities do not tolerate illegal immigration. Do not overstay your visa or violate the terms of entry. Even if you overstay for just a few days, you can be:"]},{"k":"li","s":["fined"]},{"k":"li","s":["detained"]},{"k":"li","s":["deported to the UK at your own cost"]},{"k":"li","s":["banned from returning to Malaysia"]},{"k":"p","s":["Detention facilities can be poor with limited healthcare. The British High Commission is unable to help you stay in Malaysia or issue any document to support your overstay."]},{"k":"p","s":["Keep a copy of your passport and visa safe in case your passport is lost. It may take time to get a replacement visa. If you cannot show your original visa or entry stamp on exit, you may be treated as an overstayer."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Submitting an arrival card"]},{"k":"p","s":["You must ",["submit an arrival card online","https://imigresen-online.imi.gov.my/mdac/main"]," to enter Malaysia. Do this before you travel – airlines may ask for it at check-in. If you are not passing through immigration, you do not need to complete an arrival card. See the ",["Malaysian Immigration website","https://imigresen-online.imi.gov.my/mdac/main"]," for further information, including exemption details."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Entering East Malaysia"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re travelling between Peninsular Malaysia and East Malaysia (sometimes known as Malaysian Borneo and made up of the states of Sabah and Sarawak), you must carry your passport. You will pass through immigration control and must get entry and exit stamps."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["To enter Malaysia, you must have a certificate to prove you’ve had a yellow fever vaccination if you’re coming from a ",["country listed as a transmission risk","https://nathnacyfzone.org.uk/factsheet/65/countries-with-risk-of-yellow-fever-transmission"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["For full details about medical entry requirements and recommended vaccinations, see ",["TravelHealthPro’s Malaysia guide","https://www.travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/137/malaysia#Vaccine_Recommendations"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Drug screening"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Malaysian authorities could ask you to take a urine test on arrival if they suspect drug use. If they find drugs in your system, they could detain or deport you and ban you from returning to Malaysia. You could face these penalties even if you took the drug outside Malaysia, including in a country where it is legal."]},{"k":"h","s":["Customs rules"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are strict rules about ",["goods you can take into or out of Malaysia","https://www.customs.gov.my/en/individu/pengembara/travelers-guide"],". You must declare anything that may be prohibited or subject to tax or duty."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are not sure if it is legal to bring items, including medications, declare them on entry."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Importing firearms"]},{"k":"p","s":["It’s illegal to import unlicensed firearms and ammunition into Malaysia. This includes spent ammunition and souvenirs from war museums. Do not take any weapons or replica weapons into Malaysia. You may be detained and charged if you do. If you’re convicted of dealing in illegal firearms, you can get a death sentence."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Taking money into or out of Malaysia"]},{"k":"p","s":["When you enter or exit Malaysia, you must declare cash in any currency or travellers cheques worth 10,000 US dollars or more. If you do not, customs officials could seize your money. You could get a fine of up to 3 million Malaysian ringgit and up to 5 years in prison."]},{"k":"p","s":["Before you travel, you must get approval to take ringgit worth 10,000 US dollars or more into or out of Malaysia."]},{"k":"p","s":["See the Royal Malaysian Customs Department website for more information."]},{"k":"h","s":["Safety and security"]},{"k":"p","s":["This guide also has safety advice for ",["regions of Malaysia","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/malaysia/regional-risks"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Terrorism"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a high threat of terrorist attack globally affecting UK interests and British nationals, including from groups and individuals who view the UK and British nationals as targets. Stay aware of your surroundings at all times."]},{"k":"p","s":["UK Counter Terrorism Policing has information and advice on ",["staying safe","https://www.counterterrorism.police.uk/runhidetell/"]," and what to do in the event of a terrorist attack. Find out ",["how to reduce your risk from terrorism while abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/reduce-your-risk-from-terrorism-while-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Terrorism in Malaysia"]},{"k":"p","s":["Terrorist attacks in Malaysia cannot be ruled out."]},{"k":"p","s":["Terrorism attacks could be indiscriminate, including in places visited by foreign nationals, such as:"]},{"k":"li","s":["bars and restaurants"]},{"k":"li","s":["hotels"]},{"k":"li","s":["markets"]},{"k":"li","s":["shopping malls"]},{"k":"li","s":["tourist attractions"]},{"k":"li","s":["places of worship"]},{"k":"li","s":["airports"]},{"k":"p","s":["You should be especially vigilant and maintain a high level of security awareness in crowded places and at large gatherings."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Terrorist kidnap"]},{"k":"p","s":["In Malaysia, there is a threat to foreigners of kidnapping in coastal areas of eastern Sabah from terrorist groups. The threat is particularly high on islands close to the Sulu Archipelago in the southern Philippines."]},{"k":"p","s":["The Abu Sayyaf Group (ASG), based in the Philippines, has previously kidnapped foreigners from the east coast of mainland Sabah, the islands and the surrounding waters. The group has murdered several hostages. Some groups operating in the southern Philippines, including factions of ASG, have pledged allegiance to Daesh and are likely to regard westerners as legitimate targets."]},{"k":"p","s":["British nationals are seen as legitimate targets, including tourists, humanitarian aid workers, journalists and business travellers. If you are kidnapped, the reason for your presence is unlikely to protect you or secure your safe release."]},{"k":"p","s":["The long-standing policy of the British government is not to make substantive concessions to hostage takers. The British government considers that paying ransoms and releasing prisoners builds the capability of terrorist groups and finances their activities. This can, in turn, increase the risk of further hostage-taking. The Terrorism Act (2000) makes payments to terrorists illegal."]},{"k":"h","s":["Political situation"]},{"k":"p","s":["In response to the current situation in Israel and Palestine, there have been peaceful demonstrations in some Malaysian cities, including outside some western embassies."]},{"k":"p","s":["It’s illegal under Malaysian law for foreign nationals to take part in demonstrations or protests."]},{"k":"p","s":["Monitor local and international media and avoid all demonstrations. Police can use tear gas and water cannons to control public protests."]},{"k":"h","s":["Crime"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Protecting yourself and your belongings"]},{"k":"p","s":["Bag-snatching is common, particularly in major cities. Thieves on motorbikes can target tourists. They can cut straps or pull off bags carried on the shoulder closest to the road. They have sometimes pulled people to the ground and killed or injured them if they try to hold onto their bags."]},{"k":"p","s":["To protect yourself from petty crime, avoid carrying valuables and take particular care of your passport while:"]},{"k":"li","s":["walking"]},{"k":"li","s":["in cafes"]},{"k":"li","s":["on aircraft"]},{"k":"li","s":["in airport and railway terminals"]},{"k":"li","s":["at hotels"]},{"k":"p","s":["Credit card and ATM fraud is widespread. Take care when using your card."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Personal security"]},{"k":"p","s":["There have been assaults and robberies, particularly around bars and nightlife areas. Do not open your hotel room door to strangers, especially late at night. This applies particularly to women travelling alone."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Drink spiking"]},{"k":"p","s":["There have been cases of strangers offering tourists a spiked drink, even in reputable bars or restaurants, and then robbing or assaulting them."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Methanol poisoning"]},{"k":"p","s":["There has been an increase in cases of serious illness caused by alcoholic drinks containing methanol in popular travel destinations around the world."]},{"k":"p","s":["Even small amounts of methanol can kill. It is not possible to identify methanol in alcoholic drinks by taste or smell. See ",["Travel Aware Drink Spiking and Methanol Poisoning","https://travelaware.campaign.gov.uk/spiking-and-methanol-poisoning/"]," for information about how to reduce the risks."]},{"k":"p","s":["Seek urgent medical attention if you or someone you are travelling with show the ",["signs of methanol poisoning","https://travelaware.campaign.gov.uk/spiking-and-methanol-poisoning/"]," after drinking."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Police reports"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re a victim of crime, inform the local police and get a police report for insurance purposes."]},{"k":"h","s":["Laws and cultural differences"]},{"k":"p","s":["Malaysia is a multicultural, majority Muslim country. Respect local laws, cultures, traditions and religions."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re a Muslim, you may be subject to local Sharia law."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Personal ID"]},{"k":"p","s":["By law you must carry ID. Always carry your passport."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Dress code"]},{"k":"p","s":["Dress modestly, particularly in conservative and rural areas. You may be refused entry to places of worship or government offices if you do not have your shoulders and knees covered or are not wearing closed shoes. Check the signs displayed at the entrance."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Ramadan"]},{"k":"p","s":["Ramadan is a holy month for Muslims. The dates vary by year and country. In Malaysia, Ramadan will take place in March 2025."]},{"k":"p","s":["During this time, you should:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check opening hours of shops and restaurants"]},{"k":"li","s":["follow local dress codes – clothing that does not meet local dress codes may cause more offence at this time"]},{"k":"li","s":["be aware of the risks of dehydration and that fasting can cause tiredness, particularly during the later afternoon and early evening"]},{"k":"p","s":["Get more advice when you arrive from your tour guide, hotel or business contacts."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Cannabis and drug offences"]},{"k":"p","s":["British Nationals have been arrested for attempting to take cannabis through Malaysian airports and border crossings. You can be arrested and prosecuted even if the drugs belong to someone else or if you are carrying items on behalf of another person."]},{"k":"p","s":["Never carry packages, bags or other items through Malaysian airports or border crossings for people you do not know or trust. You are responsible for the content of your luggage and personal belongings."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are arrested or detained for a drug-related offence, the British High Commission can provide consular assistance but cannot intervene in the Malaysian legal process or secure your release from custody."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Illegal drugs penalties"]},{"k":"p","s":["Possession, trafficking and manufacture of any illegal drugs are serious offences in Malaysia. This includes cannabis. British nationals have been caught carrying cannabis while transiting through Malaysia."]},{"k":"p","s":["Airports in Malaysia have security equipment and procedures in place for scanning and detecting illegal substances, including cannabis. This is also used to scan all carry-on and checked-in baggage of transiting passengers."]},{"k":"p","s":["The Malaysian authorities have a zero-tolerance policy to drug offences. Possessing drugs, even in very small quantities, carries severe penalties, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["arrest and detention"]},{"k":"li","s":["a long jail sentence if charged and convicted"]},{"k":"li","s":["a possible whipping"]},{"k":"li","s":["a possible death sentence by hanging for drug trafficking"]},{"k":"p","s":["Legal processes before court hearings can be long and expensive. It can take many months for a case to go to trial and time served ahead of that will not be included as part of the sentence."]},{"k":"p","s":["Criminal gangs in the UK and elsewhere are known to coerce people into carrying drugs across borders. Do not allow yourself to be persuaded."]},{"k":"p","s":["It is important to be aware of your rights if you are detained by the police. See our ",["guidance for those detained or imprisoned in Malaysia","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/malaysia-prisoner-pack/information-pack-for-british-nationals-detained-or-imprisoned-in-malaysia"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Police often raid venues known to be frequented by foreigners. You may have to take a urine or blood test if there is a reasonable suspicion you have used drugs. You will be arrested if the test is positive."]},{"k":"hs","s":["LGBT+ travellers"]},{"k":"p","s":["Same-sex sexual acts are illegal in Malaysia under federal law. The Malaysian authorities sometimes carry out raids on LGBT+ spaces and events. Showing affection in public could attract negative responses from other members of the public. Openly LGBT+ safe spaces and support groups exist."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more ",["advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Outdoor activities and adventure tourism"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Watersports and scuba diving"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you rent jet skis or any other type of watersports equipment, make sure adequate safety precautions are in place. Only use licensed operators, insist on training before use and make sure you’re covered by your insurance."]},{"k":"p","s":["Diving school standards are not always as high as in the UK. You should:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check a dive operator’s credentials"]},{"k":"li","s":["make sure safety equipment is available on the boat, particularly oxygen"]},{"k":"li","s":["ask about safety precautions, including the ability to transfer divers to a hyperbaric chamber"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you have not had any previous diving experience:"]},{"k":"li","s":["ask your dive instructor to explain what cover they offer before signing up"]},{"k":"li","s":["check what to do if something goes wrong, including how to call for help while at sea"]},{"k":"p","s":["The diving sites of HMS Repulse and HMS Prince of Wales, which lie in international waters off Kuantan, have been declared ‘protected places’ under the Protection of Military Remains Act 1986. Only dive there on a ‘look not touch’ basis. Make sure any boat operator you use holds a licence for diving at the wreck site."]},{"k":"p","s":["See ⁠ ",["watersports safety advice","https://www.rospa.com/leisure-water-safety/water/advice/watersports-abroad"]," from the Royal Society for the Prevention of Accidents (ROSPA)."]},{"k":"h","s":["Transport risks"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are planning to drive in Malaysia, see ",["information on driving abroad","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["You’ll need both the ",["1949 international driving permit (IDP)","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad/international-driving-permit"]," and your UK driving licence to drive in Malaysia. You cannot buy an IDP outside the UK, so get one before you travel. You must buy a new IDP for each additional year you plan to drive in Malaysia."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re in a road accident, it’s illegal to leave the scene before the police arrive. If a crowd gathers, it may be safer to go directly to the nearest police station instead."]},{"k":"p","s":["Drivers, particularly motorbike riders, do not always stop at traffic lights or pedestrian crossings."]},{"k":"p","s":["There have been several fatal bus crashes, particularly on overnight journeys. Choose a reputable operator for your journey."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you rent a motorbike, it’s illegal not to wear a helmet."]},{"k":"p","s":["Driving under the influence of alcohol is a serious offence, and the traffic police regularly carry out breath tests. If you are found driving over the legal limit, you may get:"]},{"k":"li","s":["a heavy fine"]},{"k":"li","s":["a prison sentence"]},{"k":"li","s":["deported"]},{"k":"p","s":["Road conditions in East Malaysia are not as good as those in Peninsular Malaysia."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Using taxis"]},{"k":"p","s":["At airports you pay for your taxi with a fixed-price coupon you buy at a counter in the terminal. In other areas, taxis should use a meter."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Sea and river travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["Some passenger boats have sunk due to overloading and poor maintenance. Take care when travelling by passenger ferry or speedboat. Avoid travelling on vessels that are clearly overloaded or in poor condition. Make sure life jackets are available."]},{"k":"p","s":["Piracy in South-East Asian waters is an ongoing problem. There have been attacks against ships in and around Malaysian waters, particularly in the Strait of Malacca and the waters between Sabah and the southern Philippines. Be vigilant and take appropriate precautions. Reduce opportunities for theft, establish secure areas on board and report all incidents to the coastal and flag state authorities."]},{"k":"h","s":["Extreme weather and natural disasters"]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out what you can do to ",["prepare for and respond to extreme weather and natural hazards","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/tropical-cyclones"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Seasonal storms affect Malaysia. The timing of these storms depends on location in the country. Flooding and landslides are common and can cause significant damage to infrastructure. Essential services can be interrupted. For information on local weather, check the ",["Malaysian Meteorological Department","https://www.met.gov.my/en/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Earthquakes can happen in Malaysia but are rare."]},{"k":"h","s":["Regional risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["This section has safety advice for regions of Malaysia. It only covers regions where FCDO has specific advice."]},{"k":"p","s":["You should also read ",["FCDO’s overall travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/malaysia"]," and ",["safety and security advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/malaysia/safety-and-security"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Eastern Sabah coast"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all but essential travel to all islands and dive sites off the coast of eastern Sabah from Sandakan to Tawau, including Lankayan Island, due to the threat of kidnapping. This does not apply to the mainland of Sabah."]},{"k":"p","s":["Commercial shipping companies have been advised to adopt heightened vigilance when navigating the Sulu and Celebes seas. Most maritime incidents occur in the Sulu Sea in the area between Sabah (Malaysia) and Mindanao, the Sulu Islands and Palawan (Philippines)."]},{"k":"p","s":["The Regional Co-operation Agreement on Combatting Piracy and Armed Robbery against Ships in Asia (ReCAAP) advises all ships to re-route from the area where possible."]},{"k":"h","s":["Health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Before you travel check that:"]},{"k":"li","s":["your destination can provide the healthcare you may need"]},{"k":"li","s":["you have ",["appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"]," for local treatment or unexpected medical evacuation"]},{"k":"p","s":["This is particularly important if you have a health condition or are pregnant."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency medical number"]},{"k":"p","s":["Call 999 and ask for an ambulance."]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your insurance company quickly if you’re referred to a medical facility for treatment."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine recommendations and health risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["At least 8 weeks before your trip:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check the latest ",["vaccine recommendations for Malaysia","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/137/malaysia#Vaccine_Recommendations"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see where to get vaccines and whether you have to pay on the ",["NHS travel vaccinations page","https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/travel-vaccinations/"]]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["what health risks you’ll face in Malaysia","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/137/malaysia#General_Information"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["malaria"]},{"k":"li","s":["dengue"]},{"k":"li","s":["rabies in the states of Perlis, Kedah, Penang and Sarawak"]},{"k":"p","s":["Altitude sickness is a risk in parts of Malaysia, including Mount Kinabalu in eastern Malaysia (Malaysian Borneo). Read more about ",["altitude sickness on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/26/altitude-illness"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Air pollution"]},{"k":"p","s":["High levels of air pollution in major towns and cities in Malaysia may make respiratory conditions worse."]},{"k":"p","s":["Forest fires in Indonesia can cause haze between June and October. Haze can cause disruption to local travel, regional air travel and to schools."]},{"k":"p","s":["Monitor the Air Pollutant Index Management System (APIMS) updates and health advisories issued by the ",["Malaysian Department of Environment","https://www.doe.gov.my/en/utama-english/"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Medication"]},{"k":"p","s":["The legal status and regulation of some medicines prescribed or bought in the UK can be different in other countries."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["best practice when travelling with medicines on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/43/medicines-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Healthcare in Malaysia"]},{"k":"p","s":["Government and private hospitals charge for all services. Private care is expensive. Make sure you have adequate travel health insurance and accessible funds to cover the cost of any medical treatment abroad and repatriation."]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has a ",["list of medical providers in Malaysia","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/malaysia-list-of-medical-facilities/list-of-medical-facilities-in-malaysia"]," where some staff will speak English."]},{"k":"p","s":["There is also ",["guidance on healthcare if you’re living in Malaysia","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/living-in-malaysia#healthcare-in-malaysia"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travel and mental health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["FCDO guidance on travel and mental health","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-advice-for-people-with-mental-health-issues"],". There is also ",["mental health guidance on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/85/travelling-with-mental-health-conditions"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Getting help"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) cannot provide tailored advice for individual trips. Read this travel advice and carry out your own research before deciding whether to travel."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency services in Malaysia"]},{"k":"p","s":["Police and ambulance: 999"]},{"k":"p","s":["Fire: 994"]},{"k":"p","s":["Ambulance (from a mobile): 112"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contact your travel provider and insurer"]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your travel provider and your insurer if you are involved in a serious incident or emergency abroad. They will tell you if they can help and what you need to do."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Refunds and changes to travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["For refunds or changes to travel, contact your travel provider. You may also be able to make a claim through insurance. However, insurers usually require you to talk to your travel provider first."]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out more about ",["changing or cancelling travel plans","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice#changing-or-cancelling-travel-plans"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["where to get advice if you are in a dispute with a provider"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to access previous versions of travel advice to support a claim"]},{"k":"h","s":["Support from FCDO"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has guidance on staying safe and what to do if you need help or support abroad, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["finding English-speaking ",["lawyers","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/lawyers?country=Malaysia"],", ",["funeral directors","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/funeral-directors?country=Malaysia"]," and ",["translators and interpreters","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/translators-interpreters?country=Malaysia"]," in Malaysia"]},{"k":"li","s":["dealing with a ",["death in Malaysia","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/what-to-do-after-a-british-person-dies-in-malaysia"]]},{"k":"li","s":["being ",["arrested or imprisoned in Malaysia","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/malaysia-prisoner-pack"]]},{"k":"li","s":["getting help if you’re a ",["victim of crime","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/victim-of-crime-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["what to do if you’re ",["in hospital","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/in-hospital-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["if you’re ",["affected by a crisis","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/how-to-deal-with-a-crisis-overseas"],", such as a terrorist attack"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contacting FCDO"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help abroad"]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["how to get help from the UK government abroad","https://www.contact.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/posts/malaysia/british-high-commission-kuala-lumpur"]," including in an emergency."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help in the UK"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can call FCDO in London if you need urgent help because something has happened to a friend or relative abroad."]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 020 7008 5000 (24 hours)"]},{"k":"p","s":[["Find out about call charges","https://www.gov.uk/call-charges"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/malaysia/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Risk information for British companies"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Overseas Business Risk service offers ",["information and advice for British companies operating in Malaysia","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/overseas-business-risk-malaysia"]," on how to manage political, economic, and business security-related risks."]}],"chars":23189,"change":"New information about cannabis and drug offences (‘Warnings and insurance’ page)."},"https://theconversation.com/how-did-wire-replacements-ground-planes-across-the-entire-us-296658":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-17T17:03Z","sha":"4dbca99c1f7899426f96ba812c9aeee33c16eab228e3b3b3b32640d57860e140","read":"https://theconversation.com/how-did-wire-replacements-ground-planes-across-the-entire-us-296658","bytes":177586,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"How did ‘wire replacements’ ground planes across the entire US?","by":[{"n":"Graham Wild","r":"Aviation Coordinator, Australian Defence Force Academy; UNSW"}],"pub":"2026-11-15T03:40:05Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["On Friday, all flights across the United States were grounded for more than four hours due to what appeared to be a ",["telecommunications issue","https://abcnews.com/US/timeline-faa-nationwide-ground-stop-developed/story?id=138141388"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["The Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) issued an alert citing “ ",["extraordinary circumstances","https://www.fly.faa.gov/adv/adv_otherdis?adv_date=11132026&advn=111"],"” and called for all departing flights to remain ground stopped until further notice. According to media reports, ",["about 3,500 flights","https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cqx2wyvyr3zzo"]," were in the air when the alert was issued – all landed safely."]},{"k":"p","s":["With thousands of flights delayed and cancelled, millions of passengers were stranded in airports. The travel disruptions have been slowly resolving over the weekend."]},{"k":"p","s":["The ground stop was attributed to an ",["update to air traffic control equipment","https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/nov/14/faa-ground-stop-flights-resume-airport-chaos"]," during planned maintenance. So what exactly happened?"]},{"k":"h","s":["What caused it?"]},{"k":"p","s":["US Transportation Secretary Sean Duffy attributed the disruption to maintenance undertaken by ",["government contractor L3Harris at a remote FAA facility","https://www.transportation.gov/briefing-room/icymi-us-transportation-secretary-sean-p-duffy-provides-update-restoration-airspace"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["The work involved replacing copper telecommunications wires with fibre-optic links, as well as changing how the system sends signals from an older method known as time-division multiplexing (TDM) to the more recent internet protocol (IP)."]},{"k":"p","s":["During this maintenance, a router (a networking device) reportedly generated ",["more than 1,000 connection requests","https://edition.cnn.com/2026/11/14/politics/faa-ground-stop-what-we-know"]," every four to five seconds. This affected other routers on the network, disrupting aircraft communications and some radar data feeds."]},{"k":"p","s":["Duffy described the upgrade as a ",["transition from analogue to digital technology","https://www.reuters.com/business/aerospace-defense/us-aviation-shutdown-blamed-l3-harris-installed-router-2026-11-14/"],", but this conflates different aspects of telecommunications."]},{"k":"p","s":["Copper and fibre describe the physical medium carrying signals, while TDM and IP describe how information is transmitted and organised. Copper cables can carry digital data, and TDM is widely used in digital telecommunications. Fibre-optic systems also carry digital data, using light rather than electrical signals."]},{"k":"p","s":["Consequently, replacing copper with fibre doesn’t necessarily mean a change from analogue to digital."]},{"k":"p","s":["The more significant change is the method for sending signals. With TDM, the system sends information through a single channel at fixed time slots, a bit like letting through traffic across a single-lane bridge at set intervals."]},{"k":"p","s":["By contrast, an IP network can carry information in packets along many paths, which improves flexibility and how much data the network can handle."]},{"k":"p","s":["However, it also changes how the equipment interacts – and what can happen in case of a fault. If not isolated in time, a malfunctioning router can affect other equipment connected to the same network. On fibre, excessive connection requests will travel more freely."]},{"k":"h","s":["Why would a network disruption ground planes?"]},{"k":"p","s":["Air traffic controllers use surveillance and radio communications to make sure planes travel smoothly and stay out of each other’s way."]},{"k":"p","s":["Radar and satellite-based surveillance provide aircraft positions, while communications allow controllers to issue instructions and coordinate movements between control centres."]},{"k":"p","s":["All this relies on telecommunications networks connecting facilities across the country. Aircraft can navigate independently during a network failure, but controllers can’t safely manage the usual volume of flights if the comms are not fully reliable."]},{"k":"p","s":["The precise cause of why the router sent so many requests ",["remains under investigation","https://www.wjsu.org/top-stories-from-npr/2026-11-14/faa-blames-fridays-ground-stop-on-equipment-issue-during-telecoms-network-upgrade"],". Incorrect installation, configuration errors, and defective equipment are all potential explanations, and we’ll have to wait and see."]},{"k":"p","s":["A nationwide ground stop following a technical failure is not unprecedented. In January 2023, a malfunction of the FAA’s Notice to Air Missions system, which supplies pilots with operational information, ",["also suspended departures across the United States","https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-26-107992"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Although these two failures involved different systems, both show that a national aviation network that shares services throughout can be broadly impacted in case of a fault."]},{"k":"h","s":["Was this related to Microsoft or AI?"]},{"k":"p","s":["The incident prompted speculation about it being linked to ",["the global Microsoft outage","https://theconversation.com/microsoft-has-suffered-a-global-outage-a-tech-expert-explains-what-went-wrong-296588"]," that occurred just the day before."]},{"k":"p","s":["Another suggestion was that the fault might lay with the FAA’s ",["recently announced expansion of SMART","https://avweb.com/aviation-news/faa-nationwide-ground-stop-lifts-smart-ai/"],", an artificial intelligence-assisted traffic management system."]},{"k":"p","s":["Both Microsoft and Air Space Intelligence, the company responsible for SMART, ",["have denied involvement","https://thehill.com/policy/transportation/6220416-faa-smart-ai-ground-stop-denial/"],". Officials also noted that the maintenance of the network was completely separate from this air traffic control modernisation program."]},{"k":"h","s":["What can be done to prevent this happening again?"]},{"k":"p","s":["In September 2026, ",["a US government report","https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-26-107992"]," classified 51 of the FAA’s 138 air traffic control systems as unsustainable, and another 54 as potentially unsustainable. The FAA is actively working on replacing ageing communications, radar, and control equipment."]},{"k":"p","s":["However, modernisation introduces additional risks. When you add a new technology to the system, not only do you need to fit it with existing infrastructure, but you also need to maintain operations."]},{"k":"p","s":["To prevent ground stops like the one this week, the FAA will need to do more than replacing cables or purchasing equipment. If they segment the network, faults won’t travel as far. With proper configuration, they can ensure communications don’t get overloaded if a similar router error were to happen."]},{"k":"p","s":["The ground stop was a good measure to ensure safety when the comms failed. But a resilient aviation system must not only respond safely to equipment failures, but also prevent localised faults from disrupting national operations."]}],"chars":5422},"https://theconversation.com/what-really-drove-nzs-election-overhang-and-what-can-be-done-about-it-296144":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-17T17:03Z","sha":"7bfe1162da5c2df74cf6fe172d27ee1653ac1a2978c9bf239d18a8d53d52a7e4","read":"https://theconversation.com/what-really-drove-nzs-election-overhang-and-what-can-be-done-about-it-296144","bytes":178865,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"What really drove NZ’s election overhang and what can be done about it","by":[{"n":"Jack Vowles","r":"Professor of Political Science, Te Herenga Waka — Victoria University of Wellington"}],"pub":"2026-11-09T22:19:24Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["Based on ",["provisional election results","https://electionresults.govt.nz/"],", New Zealand’s parliament could grow by up to 11 ",["overhang seats","https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/2408208/explainer-what-is-an-overhang-and-how-could-it-change-the-election-tonight"]," – without accurately representing the proportion of party votes cast."]},{"k":"p","s":["There are now growing calls for reform of the mixed member proportional ( ",["MMP","https://elections.nz/democracy-in-nz/what-is-new-zealands-system-of-government/what-is-mmp"],") electoral system adopted after the 1993 referendum to avoid undermining the legitimacy of future elections."]},{"k":"p","s":["MMP is ",["based on two votes","https://elections.nz/democracy-in-nz/what-is-new-zealands-system-of-government/what-is-mmp"],": one for a party and one for an electorate candidate. The party vote is intended to shape the overall composition of parliament, topping up seats from party lists after the electorate contests have been determined."]},{"k":"p","s":["Overhang seats are added to parliament when a party wins more electorates than its party vote share entitlement. But for the party vote to have enough seats for this process to deliver proportional representation, there need to be enough list seats available."]},{"k":"p","s":["In 1986, the ",["Royal Commission on the Electoral System","https://elections.nz/democracy-in-nz/what-is-new-zealands-system-of-government/report-of-the-royal-commission-on-the-electoral-system"]," recommended a list to electorate ratio of 60 seats each. But parliament didn’t follow the recommendation and created 65 electorate seats and only 55 list seats for the first MMP election in 1996."]},{"k":"p","s":["The ratio was not fixed because the number of general electorate seats was set to maintain 16 seats in the South Island. As a result, uneven population growth between the north and south islands led to an increasing number of electorate seats and fewer list seats."]},{"k":"p","s":["By the 2020 election, there were 72 electorate seats and only 48 list seats. In 2026, because of population growth in the South Island, the number of electorate seats went down slightly to 71 and list seats rose to 49."]},{"k":"p","s":["The ",["Electoral Act","https://www.legislation.govt.nz/act/public/1993/87/en/latest/#DLM307519"]," acknowledges the possibility that parties might win more electorate seats than their party vote entitlement. In that case, the number of list seats rises but the calculation to ensure proportionality remains based on a 120-seat parliament."]},{"k":"p","s":["For example, the Green party was assigned 16 seats on a party vote of 13.3%. But this calculation is made on a baseline of 120 seats, even though parliament will increase to adjust for the overhang of 11 seats. In a 131-seat parliament, the Greens’ seat share represents only 12.2%."]},{"k":"p","s":["A baseline of 131 seats would give the Greens an extra seat – but also increase the size of parliament to 132. Applying the same formula to the other parties not benefiting from overhang would further increase the list seats and the size of parliament."]},{"k":"p","s":["The baseline seat total would have to rise accordingly as other parties entitlements were calculated."]},{"k":"h","s":["Germany’s amendments to MMP"]},{"k":"p","s":["Germany, whose MMP electoral system was the model for New Zealand’s, also encountered this problem as votes for smaller parties rose and larger parties fell, and overhangs became more prevalent."]},{"k":"p","s":["The country’s constitutional court required an amendment to more fully compensate for overhang. But this led to a ",["huge increase in the number of seats","https://www.rnz.co.nz/national/programmes/middayreport/audio/2019058415/sven-siefken-on-germany-s-experience-with-mmp-overhangs"]," in the German Bundestag, its lower House of Parliament."]},{"k":"p","s":["In 2023, the law was amended again to cap the size of the house and simply exclude candidates elected in overhang seats. They were ranked by vote share, with those with the lowest share losing their seats. This can leave those electorates unrepresented in the Bundestag."]},{"k":"p","s":["Two reviews of New Zealand’s MMP system have recommended abolition of overhang seats, but not in a way that could restore proportionality. In fact, this solution would further reduce it."]},{"k":"p","s":["The ",["2012 Electoral Commission’s review","https://elections.nz/assets/2012-report-of-the-Electoral-Commission-on-the-review-of-mmp.pdf"]," and the ",["2023 Independent Electoral Review Committee","https://consultations.justice.govt.nz/policy/independent-electoral-review/"]," both proposed the size of parliament should be retained at 120 even if parties gained more electorate seats than entitled by their party vote. The number of list seats would go down, thus further reducing their compensatory role."]},{"k":"p","s":["This reform could consequently enhance the incentives that promote overhang strategies, encouraging strategic voting that could reduce proportionality even more."]},{"k":"p","s":["Te Pāti Māori could continue to employ its electorate-vote-only strategy and efforts to promote vote splitting on the right could intensify."]},{"k":"h","s":["Larger parliament, more list seats"]},{"k":"p","s":["An increase in the number of list seats would go part way to addressing the problem."]},{"k":"p","s":["The National Party’s provisional overhang of about seven seats is unlikely to have been generated by strategic voting intended to subvert proportionality, except on the very margins."]},{"k":"p","s":["It is more likely an artefact of a population distribution across electorates that concentrates National votes and disperses Labour votes. Campaigning by Green candidates that split left electorate votes, and the incumbency advantages of National’s many sitting electorate MPs, would also have had an effect."]},{"k":"p","s":["National’s overhang would probably be almost gone if there were 140 seats in parliament, with matching electorate and list seats of 70 each – the original half-and-half ratio proposed by the Royal Commission."]},{"k":"p","s":["But this would not address the strategy employed by Te Pāti Māori, or more aggressive and persistent strategies on the right that would almost certainly continue, if only to try to offset that of Te Pāti Māori."]},{"k":"p","s":["Germany’s solution to the problem almost completely destroys the incentives for the active pursuit of overhang. But it means some electorate candidates initially elected on low vote shares don’t sit in parliament and their electorates are not directly represented."]},{"k":"p","s":["A unique variation of this model might allow those candidates to sit as MPs, but their party would only be able to cast party votes in the house consistent with its party vote share entitlement. Most significant votes in parliament are now conducted through this process."]},{"k":"p","s":["A more dramatic solution would be to fuse party and electorate votes, thus moving to a one-vote version of the MMP system. But this would destroy one of the merits of New Zealand’s current model, which allows us to distinguish between our local MP and the party they represent."]},{"k":"p","s":["Whatever the case, any changes to MMP will require decisions to be made in government and within and between political parties, all of whom have incentives to maintain their power and positions."]},{"k":"p","s":["But we would all benefit from a system that ensures fair and proportional electoral outcomes that can be accepted as legitimate representations of public preferences."]}],"chars":6181},"https://theconversation.com/nz-will-have-a-caretaker-government-for-weeks-what-that-means-and-how-it-works-295750":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-17T17:18Z","sha":"31c2d7ee5dbfda9fa6f777c16f3923efd248d253135efa7bc694427bdceddbe3","read":"https://theconversation.com/nz-will-have-a-caretaker-government-for-weeks-what-that-means-and-how-it-works-295750","bytes":179481,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"NZ will have a caretaker government for weeks. What that means and how it works","by":[{"n":"Grant Duncan","r":"Research Associate, Public Policy Institute, University of Auckland, Waipapa Taumata Rau"}],"pub":"2026-11-08T01:04:53Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["New Zealand’s provisional election results have created considerable uncertainty, and may feel frustrating for many voters. But fortunately there are guidelines for the parties and for the process of government during this limbo period."]},{"k":"p","s":["Final results – which will include a large number of special votes – won’t be known until November 27, and it will take some weeks of negotiations between the parties before any viable coalition agreement can be made."]},{"k":"p","s":["The ",["caretaker convention","https://www.dpmc.govt.nz/our-business-units/cabinet-office/supporting-work-cabinet/cabinet-manual/6-elections-transitions-and-government-formation/caretaker-convention"]," and the guidance of the ",["Cabinet Manual","https://www.dpmc.govt.nz/our-business-units/cabinet-office/supporting-work-cabinet/cabinet-manual"]," – the “authoritative guide to central government decision making” – now come into play."]},{"k":"h","s":["How we got here"]},{"k":"p","s":["As of today, neither the left nor right is giving any ground. Opposition leader, Labour’s Chris Hipkins, did not concede defeat on election night. On the other hand, he was in no position to claim a victory either."]},{"k":"p","s":["Incumbent prime minister, National’s ",["Christopher Luxon, said","https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/2419595/election-results-christopher-luxon-says-outcome-too-close-to-call"],", “National is once again the largest party in parliament and we have a pathway to forming the next government.” He too is in no position to say much more."]},{"k":"p","s":["Thanks to a parliamentary ",["overhang caused by widespread vote splitting","https://theconversation.com/a-fractious-campaign-heralds-a-cliffhanger-election-what-to-watch-as-nz-votes-295734"],", National has 3,650 fewer votes than Labour. But National provisionally holds 42 seats to Labour’s 36. That is clearly not proportional in the way the mixed member proportional system ( ",["MMP","https://elections.nz/democracy-in-nz/what-is-new-zealands-system-of-government/what-is-mmp"],") is designed to work."]},{"k":"p","s":["However, the party with the most seats doesn’t necessarily form the next government, as we saw in 2017 when Jacinda Ardern’s Labour got over the line with the help of New Zealand First."]},{"k":"p","s":["The preliminary results have Luxon’s three party coalition (National, ACT and New Zealand First) holding 67 out of 131 seats – a narrow majority on the night."]},{"k":"p","s":["But this may be reduced once special votes are counted, meaning Hipkins still stands a chance of leading a four-party coalition (Labour, Green, Te Pāti Māori, Opportunity)."]},{"k":"p","s":["The basic goal for both sides is to command a majority of the seats in the House of Representatives. The question at the moment is who will lead that majority, and which parties will it consist of. A government rules only so long as it has the confidence of the house."]},{"k":"p","s":["So, given the cliffhanger outcome and the time it will take to resolve, what happens in the meantime?"]},{"k":"h","s":["Caretaker government in action"]},{"k":"p","s":["Under the caretaker convention, incumbent prime minister Luxon and his team of ministers remain in office. It’s not correct to say “there’s no government”. The governor general must always have responsible advisers and day-to-day administration must continue."]},{"k":"p","s":["During this caretaker period, significant decisions will be deferred or attended to with a light touch. If a national emergency arose, however, then the caretaker government would of course act, of necessity."]},{"k":"p","s":["Significant or controversial issues that can’t be deferred should be decided in consultation with other parties, to ensure the matter has the confidence of the house."]},{"k":"p","s":["How the caretaker ministers conduct themselves in this period is a matter of political judgement and attention to the decision-making context. As the ",["Cabinet Manual puts it","https://www.dpmc.govt.nz/our-business-units/cabinet-office/supporting-work-cabinet/cabinet-manual/6-elections-transitions-and-government-formation/caretaker-convention"],", “There are no hard and fast rules.”"]},{"k":"p","s":["Decision making is constrained for the time being, but the government of the country will continue."]},{"k":"p","s":["Special votes tend to lean to the left. The final count, then, may see a seat or two change hands. Wairarapa, for instance, is ",["provisionally held","https://www.nzherald.co.nz/hawkes-bay-today/news/mcanulty-v-butterick-wairarapa-election-result-in-the-balance/HEO3W24KGBE5LJFWCQGFDPNOE4/"]," by National’s Mike Butterick, but only by 139 votes."]},{"k":"p","s":["The size of the overhang (currently a record-breaking 11 seats) may also change."]},{"k":"p","s":["Those variables will affect how the parties on both sides of the house negotiate to reach agreements to form the next government. Once a workable majority is found on one side or the other, the parties in that coalition must make it clear to the public what their arrangements are."]},{"k":"p","s":["The governor general does not guide or participate in the negotiations, but appoints the ministers to the new government once it’s clear which parties have the confidence of the house."]},{"k":"h","s":["An obligation for stable government"]},{"k":"p","s":["There should be no need for a fresh election. It’s up to the parties now to find a majority. There are strong incentives for them to do so, and no party wants to be blamed for forcing the country to return to the polls."]},{"k":"p","s":["If the final numbers deprive Luxon’s incumbent coalition of a majority, commentators have mostly assumed there may be a role for the newcomer Opportunity Party. But Opportunity’s policies are more compatible with the three parties of the left."]},{"k":"p","s":["Given the party’s MPs are all new to parliament, though, it may make sense for them to support a government on a ",["confidence and supply","https://www.franksogilvie.co.nz/news/explainer-coalition-agreements"]," basis, or even by promising to abstain on confidence votes – if their support is actually needed."]},{"k":"p","s":["A majority can always be found, but that always requires some compromises – that is the point of the system. The parties are under a powerful obligation to provide a stable government – and, constitutionally, the governor general must never be without responsible advisers (ministers)."]},{"k":"p","s":["For the time being, then, there is a caretaker government and public administration carries on. The people have cast their votes to elect their representatives. It’s now up to those representatives to form the next government."]}],"chars":5168},"https://theconversation.com/south-sudan-wants-to-hold-elections-in-december-3-major-obstacles-and-some-ways-around-them-295467":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-17T17:18Z","sha":"4acb7f13a939b587a8f85fa7105eec6c849591c9761f8d24459726893bf04ad5","read":"https://theconversation.com/south-sudan-wants-to-hold-elections-in-december-3-major-obstacles-and-some-ways-around-them-295467","bytes":183788,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"South Sudan wants to hold elections in December: 3 major obstacles and some ways around them","by":[{"n":"Jan Pospisil","r":"Researcher at the Austrian Institute for International Affairs, Österreichisches Institut für Internationale Politik (OIIP)"}],"pub":"2026-10-30T16:36:32Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["South Sudan is scheduled to hold general elections ",["on 22 December 2026","https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c4gyl1n332po"]," – its first since independence. President Salva Kiir and his inner circle have repeatedly ",["insisted that the elections will take place on that date","https://eyeradio.org/envoy-adut-further-transitions-or-extensions-are-no-longer-subject-to-negotiation/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["The elections are meant to bring South Sudan’s post-conflict transition to an end. Since 2018, a power-sharing government led by the ",["South Sudan People’s Liberation Movement","https://splm.ss/about"]," (SPLM) and including the major opposition party ",["SPLM-in-Opposition","https://ucdp.uu.se/statebased/12413"]," (SPLM-IO) has been ruling the country. The election would allow for this arrangement to be replaced by a government people have voted for."]},{"k":"p","s":["The ruling party (SPLM) is already ",["conducting its internal primaries","https://eyeradio.org/splm-to-kick-off-primaries-for-parliamentary-state-and-local-positions/"]," to select aspirants."]},{"k":"p","s":["However, the electoral preparations face a serious problem."]},{"k":"p","s":["The National Elections Commission announced that ",["voter registration would begin in early November 2026","https://eyeradio.org/voter-registration-to-begin-in-early-november-nec-says/"],". This leaves less than two months before polling day."]},{"k":"p","s":["Even with accelerated preparations, the remaining tasks will be extremely difficult to complete. Three major obstacles stand in the way."]},{"k":"p","s":["Firstly, South Sudan has no existing national electoral register. The electoral authorities must still register voters, verify the register, confirm candidates, prepare ballot papers and organise polling across the country. Secondly, ",["millions of people are internally displaced","https://dtm.iom.int/south-sudan"]," and infrastructure is poor. Lastly, uncertainty remains over a ",["recently amended electoral law","https://op.gov.ss/president-kiir-assents-to-elections-act-amendment-paving-the-way-for-december-polls"]," intended to regulate the electoral process."]},{"k":"p","s":["I have ",["studied South Sudan’s post-war transition","https://scholar.google.com/citations?hl=de&user=ZOpQdSwAAAAJ&view_op=list_works&sortby=pubdate"],", and in my view the country’s challenges in the coming months extend beyond the electoral calendar. The transitional institutions established under the ",["2018 peace agreement to end a five-year civil war","https://www.peaceagreements.org/agreements/2112/"]," were ",["dissolved in September 2026","https://op.gov.ss/president-kiir-assents-to-elections-act-amendment-paving-the-way-for-december-polls"],". They were replaced by caretaker arrangements. This makes another postponement of the elections more difficult."]},{"k":"p","s":["Yet any delay would also prolong a caretaker government operating without parliamentary checks and balances. This takes any official scrutiny away from the executive."]},{"k":"h","s":["The challenges"]},{"k":"p","s":["South Sudan has an estimated population of ",["12.5 million","https://worldpopulationreview.com/countries/south-sudan"],", ",["80%","https://www.ifad.org/en/w/countries/south-sudan"]," of whom live in rural areas. The country is divided into ",["10 states and three administrative areas","https://ss.china-embassy.gov.cn/eng/sdnfjj/snfgk/201108/t20110822_7649145.htm"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Voter registration: the National Elections Commission plans to register voters manually. It will use paper registration books before entering the information into its computer systems. This process faces several challenges."]},{"k":"li","s":["Thousands of staff need to be trained. By October 2026 ",["recruitment was happening","https://nec.gov.ss/nec-invites-applications-for-state-and-administrative-area-returning-officers/"],", but ensuring the quality of the process will be a challenge."]},{"k":"li","s":["The country’s population is widely dispersed, with many communities difficult to reach because of ",["poor roads and seasonal flooding","https://www.unocha.org/publications/report/south-sudan/south-sudan-floods-snapshot-10-october-2025"],"."]},{"k":"li","s":["Millions of South Sudanese live far from their home areas, many without valid documents. Around ",["2.5 million people","https://dtm.iom.int/south-sudan"]," are internally displaced, while more than ",["2.3 million refugees","https://www.unhcr.org/emergencies/south-sudan-emergency"]," live in neighbouring countries like Kenya and Uganda. This raises questions about where and how they can register and vote. The electoral commission appears to be planning to accept ",["confirmation of people’s identities through community chiefs","https://www.sudanspost.com/nec-turns-to-chiefs-to-verify-voters-without-national-ids/"],". This would enable those without IDs to participate in the process."]},{"k":"li","s":["Polling stations need personnel and equipment."]},{"k":"p","s":["Candidate nominations: those who want elective seats must get written endorsements from registered voters for their nominations to be accepted. There is a potential appeal process if signatures are contested."]},{"k":"p","s":["Only once candidates are confirmed can the final ballot papers be prepared. These must be printed and distributed across a country where even ordinary transport remains difficult."]},{"k":"p","s":["The legal framework: Kiir ",["signed an amendment to the National Elections Act","https://op.gov.ss/president-kiir-assents-to-elections-act-amendment-paving-the-way-for-december-polls"]," on 21 September 2026. This finalised the polling procedures, the political constituencies and the size of parliament. Yet the final signed text has not been made available either publicly or to stakeholders involved in the election process."]},{"k":"p","s":["This leaves uncertainty about the precise requirements governing several stages of the process, including their sequencing."]},{"k":"h","s":["The ruling party’s electoral process"]},{"k":"p","s":["The ongoing ",["SPLM primaries","https://eyeradio.org/splm-to-kick-off-primaries-for-parliamentary-state-and-local-positions/"]," have generated remarkable political activity. Hundreds of aspirants have submitted applications for nominations, from parliamentary to governorship seats. Party members are voting to select SPLM nominees."]},{"k":"p","s":["These primaries are a ",["serious exercise","https://eyeradio.org/splm-primaries-in-greater-pibor-face-dispute-over-alleged-interference/"],". Candidates are investing substantial resources, and local party structures are politically active. In several places, competition is intense."]},{"k":"p","s":["The process has also generated allegations of manipulation and ",["disputes over candidate selection","https://eyeradio.org/ferida-says-alleged-threats-to-force-her-out-of-splm-gubernatorial-race-resolved/"],". Some contests have become confrontational."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more: ",["South Sudan has never had an election to hand over presidential power: so what are the rules of succession?","https://theconversation.com/south-sudan-has-never-had-an-election-to-hand-over-presidential-power-so-what-are-the-rules-of-succession-276640"]]},{"k":"p","s":["The SPLM is ",["dominant","https://sudantribune.com/article/40238"]," in many parts of the country. Winning its nomination may effectively determine eventual winners in several constituencies."]},{"k":"p","s":["But the primaries create another timetable problem. Candidates cannot complete formal nomination applications before the party has decided who will represent it. The ruling party’s own electoral mobilisation is, therefore, adding pressure to an already overly compressed electoral calendar."]},{"k":"h","s":["Another extension?"]},{"k":"p","s":["South Sudan has postponed elections repeatedly since signing its ",["2018 peace agreement","https://www.peaceagreements.org/media/documents/ag2112_5be57be98faf9.pdf"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Elections first scheduled for ",["December 2022","https://theconversation.com/south-sudan-has-never-had-an-election-to-hand-over-presidential-power-so-what-are-the-rules-of-succession-276640"]," were pushed to 2024. This was then moved to 2026 because of delays in implementing parts of the peace agreement, like constitutional reforms and security arrangements. These tasks remain unfulfilled."]},{"k":"p","s":["Another extension, however, would take place under very different circumstances. The September 2026 ",["constitutional amendment","https://nec.gov.ss/download/constitutional-amendment-13-2026/"]," removed the requirement to complete the permanent constitution and population census before holding elections. Kiir then ",["dissolved the transitional government and legislative institutions","https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c8750ykkglq1o"],", replacing these with caretaker arrangements."]},{"k":"p","s":["This ",["changed the political landscape","https://peacerep.org/2026/09/28/in-the-caretaker-trap-the-legal-end-of-south-sudans-transition/"],". Previous extensions preserved the transitional power-sharing institutions established under the peace agreement."]},{"k":"p","s":["The presidency has also invested considerable political authority in the December deadline. The SPLM leadership has insisted that general elections will be held at all levels. Retreating from this commitment would carry political costs."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more: ",["South Sudan’s first election won’t be perfect – but another delay could be even riskier","https://theconversation.com/south-sudans-first-election-wont-be-perfect-but-another-delay-could-be-even-riskier-287806"]]},{"k":"p","s":["With parliament dissolved, the institutional route towards another political extension is more complicated. Elections, therefore, appear to be the only available pathway towards restoring a functioning, legitimate government and legislature."]},{"k":"p","s":["There is also ",["strong public support among South Sudanese","https://eyeradio.org/survey-finds-strong-public-support-for-december-2026-elections-despite-security-concerns/"]," for elections to go ahead."]},{"k":"p","s":["The difficulty, then, is how to give electoral authorities more time without reopening the political transition or abandoning the commitment to elections."]},{"k":"h","s":["Next steps"]},{"k":"p","s":["One possibility is a technical postponement. This could provide additional time for registration and candidate nomination. It would maintain the political commitment to conducting general elections."]},{"k":"p","s":["Another possibility is technical staging. Different electoral contests – such as for the presidency or parliamentary seats – could be held several months apart while remaining within one general election process. This might ease the administrative burden without abandoning the commitment to elections."]},{"k":"p","s":["But the legal basis for such arrangements remains uncertain."]},{"k":"p","s":["The country’s caretaker arrangements were intended to administer a short electoral period. Their continuation becomes more difficult to justify if elections are postponed for several months."]}],"chars":7608},"https://theconversation.com/black-women-helped-return-brazils-lula-to-power-can-their-gains-convert-into-political-power-295727":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-18T14:24Z","sha":"d4820381581a0e11b79da86061b9857396ab95761966f45285e10c5a398a48d0","read":"https://theconversation.com/black-women-helped-return-brazils-lula-to-power-can-their-gains-convert-into-political-power-295727","bytes":188967,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Black women helped return Brazil’s Lula to power – can their gains convert into political power?","by":[{"n":"Gladys Mitchell-Walthour","r":"Dan T. Blue Endowed Chair & Professor of Political Science, North Carolina Central University"},{"n":"Kia Lilly Caldwell","r":"Professor of African & African American Studies, Washington University in St. Louis"}],"pub":"2026-11-18T13:35:17Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["The reelection of President Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva in late October 2026 was widely seen as ",["bucking the trend of ascendant right-wing populism","https://theconversation.com/brazil-lulas-presidential-win-puts-the-brakes-on-the-rise-of-the-far-right-in-latin-america-295219"]," in South America. Yet Brazil’s general election was notable for another reason: the remarkable role of Black Brazilian women voters and politicians."]},{"k":"p","s":["Black women candidates were crucial in ",["digital mobilization efforts","https://www.nytimes.com/2026/10/26/world/americas/brazil-election-lula-social-media.html"]," encouraging voters to support Lula during the first and second rounds of the election and were decisive in his victory. Meanwhile, a record ",["15 Black women","https://agenciabrasil.ebc.com.br/politica/noticia/2026-10/cresce-numero-de-mulheres-eleitas-para-o-congresso-nacional"]," will now be sworn in to Congress as federal deputies on Feb. 1, 2027, according to the government-run Agência Brasil – an increase of 67% from 2022."]},{"k":"p","s":["As scholars who have ",["analyzed Brazilian politics,","https://afas.washu.edu/people/kia-lilly-caldwell"]," ",["race and gender","https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=1Otkwn0AAAAJ&hl=en"]," for several decades, we have seen a ",["significant representation shift","https://agenciabrasil.ebc.com.br/en/politica/noticia/2026-08/rise-black-candidates-boosts-representation-brazils-diversity"]," in Brazilian politics in recent years, driven by Black women and the emergence of a Black vote in the country."]},{"k":"p","s":["The election cycles of ",["2018","https://theconversation.com/sexism-racism-drive-more-black-women-to-run-for-office-in-both-brazil-and-us-104208"]," ",["and 2020","https://theconversation.com/my-vote-will-be-black-a-wave-of-afro-brazilian-women-ran-for-office-in-2020-but-found-glass-ceiling-hard-to-break-150521"]," saw an initial surge in Black women running for, and winning, elected offices around the country that only increased in 2026."]},{"k":"p","s":["Black women have been at the forefront of anti-racist and feminist activism ",["in Brazil for five decades","https://radis.ensp.fiocruz.br/opiniao/pos-tudo/a-luta-invisivel-o-legado-e-a-resistencia-das-mulheres-negras/"],", but their increased engagement in electoral politics is more novel. It has also shifted the political terrain in Brazil in ways that will continue to reverberate in the coming years."]},{"k":"h","s":["An emerging voting bloc"]},{"k":"p","s":["Brazil’s last census, in 2022, found that ",["56% of the population was Afro-descendant","https://www.gov.br/igualdaderacial/pt-br/composicao/secretaria-de-gestao-do-sistema-nacional-de-promocao-da-igualdade-racial/diretoria-de-avaliacao-monitoramento-e-gestao-da-informacao/hub-igualdade-racial/populacao"]," when “Black” and “brown” color categories were combined. Black activists have advocated for combining these categories for decades to reflect the true size of Brazil’s Afro-descendant population, including individuals with mixed European and other ancestry."]},{"k":"p","s":["Yet despite their majority share of the population, Black Brazilians have historically been politically underrepresented. That’s especially true of Black women, ",["who at 28%","https://www1.folha.uol.com.br/internacional/en/brazil/2021/08/black-women-are-now-the-largest-group-in-brazils-public-universities.shtml"]," of the total populace make up the largest demographic group in Brazil. Even after the gains in the latest election, however, Afro-descendant politicians of all genders will only make up ",["26.3% of Brazil’s Congress","https://almapreta.com.br/sessao/politica/camara-deputados-pretos-pardos-2027/"]," – a 2.9% decline from 2023. Despite their gains in the lower house of the Brazilian legislature, moreover, no Black women will serve come February in Brazil’s Senate."]},{"k":"p","s":["Indeed, in the past, only a handful of Black women have served in office at the city, state or federal level. Those that have include pioneering political figures like ",["Maria Olivia Santana","https://www.humanity-summit.com/team-member/maria-olivia-santana/"]," – who since 2005 has served as a city councilor in Salvador and state representative for Bahia — and ",["Benedita da Silva","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedita_da_Silva"],", a longtime congresswoman and former senator from Rio de Janeiro who on Nov. 17 ",["announced her retirement","https://oglobo.globo.com/politica/noticia/2026/11/16/aos-84-anos-benedita-da-silva-anuncia-que-nao-disputara-novas-eleicoes-entrei-com-lula-saio-com-ele.ghtml"]," from politics. Yet despite their lack of relative political power, da Silva, along with other Black women activists, was pivotal in shaping democracy in Brazil as the country emerged from dictatorship, and ",["played a leading role","https://doi.org/10.1215/00182168-11684242"]," in writing the country’s new constitution in 1988."]},{"k":"h","s":["The blossoming of ‘Marielle’s seeds’"]},{"k":"p","s":["Many of the Black women who ran for office in 2026 ran as “Marielle’s seeds” – a reference to late Rio City Councilwoman ",["Marielle Franco","https://www.frontlinedefenders.org/en/profile/marielle-franco"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["A stalwart progressive legislator and advocate for marginalized communities, Franco was assassinated on the ",["orders of two right-wing politicians","https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c4g058v3d35o"]," in March 2018, ",["sparking large protests","https://time.com/5210509/assassination-brazilian-politician-marielle-franco-global-icon/"]," throughout Brazil and solidarity movements across the globe."]},{"k":"p","s":["Her death also galvanized large numbers of Black women to engage in politics and run for office. During the 2026 election, the ",["Marielle Franco Institute","https://www.institutomariellefranco.org/en"]," – established by her family in Franco’s memory – promoted the social media campaign “Vote por Marielle” to drive support for progressive politicians."]},{"k":"p","s":["Social media campaigns have been widely credited for motivating younger voters and ",["helping Lula overturn a first-round loss","https://theconversation.com/how-lula-turned-the-tables-on-the-right-in-brazil-and-the-us-with-his-comeback-presidential-victory-294810"]," to Flávio Bolsonaro. And the role of Black voters was crucial, with the ",["main gains that propelled Lula to victory","https://historianspeaks.org/blog/the-10-million-vote-turnaround-brazil-runoff-2026#sec-the-continuity-of-afro-brazilian-agency"]," coming from urban peripheries and “favelas” – informal settlements that are home to a higher proportion of Afro-descendant Brazilians."]},{"k":"p","s":["The late votes to Lula in the second round could not stop a ",["swing to the right in the federal Congress","https://apnews.com/article/brazil-congress-right-wing-explainer-changes-9e4303e35febb01cb7dce86a4d589dc7"]," that had already been decided after the first round of voting on Oct. 5."]},{"k":"p","s":["But even so, Black voters and social media campaigns helped vote in a cross-generation cohort of Black women, most of whom are affiliated with leftist political parties."]},{"k":"p","s":["Among them is ",["Vilma Reis","https://tmc.com.br/eleicoes/candidato/vilma-reis/"],", a leading Black feminist, anti-racist militant and soon-to-be state representative in the predominantly Black northeast state of Bahia. Reis has long challenged the dominant imagery in Brazilian politics by centering aesthetic and cultural aspects of Blackness that are frequently marginalized in Brazil – such as wearing her hair in locs and highlighting her ties to the often persecuted ",["Afro-Brazilian religion Candomblé","https://www.npr.org/sections/parallels/2013/09/16/216890587/brazilian-believers-of-hidden-religion-step-out-of-shadows"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Reis will join ",["Erika Hilton","https://ranking.org.br/en/congressperson/erika-santos-silva"],", a Black transgender woman from São Paulo, reelected to the lower chamber of Brazil’s Congress. A member of the Party of Socialism and Liberty, Hilton was elected as Brazil’s first trans member of Congress in 2022 and has since pushed a pro-LGBTQ and pro-labor agenda. In fact, Hilton’s bill to institute a 40-hour work week, passed into law in October, was viewed as a ",["key victory for Lula","https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cmwy43pp08elo"]," leading up to the second round of the election, garnering widespread support from workers."]},{"k":"p","s":["Another notable Black woman elected to Congress in the October vote was 21-year-old Workers’ Party candidate ",["Ana Elisa Santos Silva","https://ground.news/article/ana-elisa-arrives-in-brasilia-to-reinforce-lulas-communication-in-the-second-round"]," from the state of Minas Gerais. Known by her first name, Ana Elisa represents a younger generation of Black women politicians. Her win was aided by social media – she had 2 million Instagram followers going into the vote, a number that swelled to 6 million after the second round of voting. She was also enlisted to help Lula’s successful social media campaign, with her own message focused on combating disinformation in social media, antiracism and defending equality."]},{"k":"p","s":["While the three women represent different generations – Reis is 57, Hilton is 33 and Silva is 21 – all ran campaigns centered on supporting communities that have been targeted by Bolsonaro and other right-wing candidates in recent years. That found a wide audience too, with Hilton and Silva receiving the ",["first- and second- highest number","https://g1.globo.com/politica/noticia/2026/11/15/bancada-feminina-congresso-perfil.ghtml"]," of votes of any women running for Congress this year."]},{"k":"h","s":["Turning representation into power"]},{"k":"p","s":["In his previous three administrations, Lula advanced anti-racist policies, including establishing a ",["federal ministry for racial equality","https://truthout.org/articles/lulas-new-government-is-taking-steps-to-combat-racism-in-brazil/"]," and promoting affirmative action in federal jobs and public universities."]},{"k":"p","s":["Given the role Black women played in securing Lula’s unprecedented fourth victory, many activists now believe the onus is on Lula’s government to respond to calls to turn greater representation into action."]},{"k":"p","s":["Organizations such as ",["Black Women Decide","https://www.aaihs.org/black-womens-political-power-and-the-future-of-democracy-in-brazil/"]," have called on Lula to appoint a ",["Black woman to the Supreme Court","https://almapreta.com.br/sessao/politica/ato-camara-ministra-negra-no-stf/"]," – something that has not happened in the court’s 135 years."]},{"k":"p","s":["This, combined with Black women’s increased presence in state- and federal-level offices, could usher in an unprecedented era of Black women’s power in politics."]}],"chars":7127},"https://theconversation.com/wars-and-cost-of-living-have-pushed-climate-change-off-the-priority-list-296984":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-20T01:14Z","sha":"a43bce92271c901d1eb4820604718c399fb388ee370ef3ef604115fb71751269","read":"https://theconversation.com/wars-and-cost-of-living-have-pushed-climate-change-off-the-priority-list-296984","bytes":183619,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Wars and cost of living have pushed climate change off the priority list","by":[{"n":"Matt McDonald","r":"Professor of International Relations, The University of Queensland"}],"pub":"2026-11-20T00:31:15Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["For the estimated 100,000 participants squashed onto crowded buses and trains or walking the streets of the Turkish Mediterranean city of Antalya, the COP31 climate summit is the only game in town."]},{"k":"p","s":["Here, preventing dangerous climate change – for most attendees at least, aside from the ",["fossil fuel lobbyists","https://theconversation.com/fossil-fuel-lobbyists-are-trying-to-derail-climate-action-once-again-at-cop31-can-they-be-stopped-294668"]," – is their motivation."]},{"k":"p","s":["But there’s a risk this United Nations climate summit in Turkey, also called Türkiye, and the issue of global warming more generally, is being crowded out of ",["public consciousness","https://theconversation.com/worries-about-climate-change-are-waning-in-many-well-off-nations-but-growing-in-turkey-brazil-and-india-269160"]," by other political issues and mercurial global tensions."]},{"k":"p","s":["What does this mean for any chance of meaningful climate action coming from the COP31 meeting?"]},{"k":"h","s":["The rollercoaster of global politics"]},{"k":"p","s":["The most significant current distraction from the climate crisis is war, with large-scale conflicts playing out in ",["Iran","https://theconversation.com/how-decades-of-us-forever-war-led-to-the-republicans-midterm-losses-296201"],", ",["Ukraine","https://theconversation.com/putin-hopes-hardship-will-force-ukrainians-to-sue-for-peace-polling-suggests-it-wont-work-294619"]," and ",["Gaza","https://theconversation.com/why-gaza-peace-deals-keep-unravelling-290072"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Indirectly, these have prompted responses that ",["may reduce climate emissions","https://theconversation.com/how-the-us-elections-and-iran-war-are-influencing-the-uns-cop31-climate-talks-and-discussions-around-fossil-fuels-296682"],". The US-Iran conflict, for example, has ",["sparked urgent discussions","https://theconversation.com/oil-shortages-escalate-from-wars-in-iran-and-ukraine-with-few-signs-of-relief-288858"]," about national energy security. And it has driven the ",["take-up of electric vehicles","https://theconversation.com/our-interest-in-electric-vehicles-has-grown-due-to-oil-price-spikes-and-its-likely-to-remain-278664"],", as consumers confront surging oil prices."]},{"k":"p","s":["But the demise of the liberal world order that has ",["enabled these conflicts","https://www.abc.net.au/listen/programs/bigideas/who-killed-the-liberal-international-order-andrew-phillips/105829696"]," also means state security concerns have come to the fore. And this has happened at the expense of global concerns ",["such as climate change","https://toda.org/global-outlooks/soaring-global-military-spending-threatens-climate-action/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["The COP31 president of negotiations, Australia, is a case in point. Australia has recently doubled down on the security partnership between Australia, the United Kingdom, and the United States, or AUKUS, which has been estimated to cost ",["A$368 billion","https://www.theguardian.com/world/2025/jun/15/aukus-will-cost-australia-368bn-what-if-there-was-a-better-cheaper-defence-strategy?CMP=Share_iOSApp_Other"],". Meanwhile, Australia’s financial commitments to climate action pale in comparison."]},{"k":"p","s":["Domestically, spending on climate action is cancelled out by spending that contributes directly to climate change. By ",["some estimates","https://wwf.org.au/news/2026/fossil-fuels-receive-four-times-more-government-support-than-renewables-new-report"],", the Australian government’s subsidies for fossil fuels outstrip its investment in renewable energy or electrification."]},{"k":"p","s":["Internationally, Australia has committed only ",["A$50 million","https://www.climatechangenews.com/2026/11/17/rich-countries-must-step-up-to-replenish-green-climate-fund-duarte-says/"]," to the Green Climate Fund. This UN-hosted financial organisation distributes funds to developing countries for greenhouse gas reduction and adaptation to climate change."]},{"k":"p","s":["Australia has pledged the same amount to the ",["Loss and Damage fund","https://acfid.asn.au/acfid-welcomes-the-australian-governments-contribution-of-50-million-to-the-loss-and-damage-fund/"],", designed to compensate developing states for unavoidable climate harms, and ",["A$100 million","https://ministers.dfat.gov.au/minister/pat-conroy/media-release/supporting-pacific-family-cop28-respond-climate-change"]," to the Pacific Resilience Facility, for climate grants in the Pacific."]},{"k":"p","s":["These are not insignificant commitments, but the prioritisation of traditional security concerns (via nuclear-powered submarines) paints a clear picture of Canberra’s priorities. And the deprioritisation of climate change is a concern."]},{"k":"h","s":["Cost of living"]},{"k":"p","s":["So where does climate change sit among the concerns of the citizens of the 196 countries and the EU represented here in Antalya?"]},{"k":"p","s":["For developing countries, it remains urgent. For Pacific residents, discussion of how the world will “ ",["overshoot” the 1.5°C target","https://theconversation.com/cop31-the-world-is-heading-for-climate-overshoot-the-un-summit-must-start-planning-for-what-comes-next-296769"]," risks sounding like fatalism – such warming would be ",["catastrophic for them","https://www.abc.net.au/pacific/programs/pacificbeat/cop31pican/107371924"],". With that in mind, Pacific voices have pushed hard for deep and sustained emissions cuts ",["here at COP31","https://forumsec.org/publications/release-pacific-political-champions-call-less-talk-and-more-action-cop-31"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["But in ",["developed countries","https://theconversation.com/worries-about-climate-change-are-waning-in-many-well-off-nations-but-growing-in-turkey-brazil-and-india-269160"],", the rise of cost-of-living concerns has pushed climate change down the domestic political agenda. They played a prominent role in recent elections in the US and New Zealand, for example, with ",["analysts noting","https://youtu.be/NE1CVMANCGk?si=Upo2c1CRoZVsnte5"]," inflation was “rampant” in developed countries across the globe."]},{"k":"p","s":["In Australia, ",["annual reports on social cohesion","https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/nov/04/social-cohesion-report-australia-cost-of-living-economy-immigration-multiculturalism-ai-ntwnfb?CMP=Share_iOSApp_Other"]," have found increasing concerns about cost of living and a relative decrease in concerns about climate change. Other polls show declining support for strong action on climate change, even while ",["climate effects worsen","https://poll.lowyinstitute.org/charts/climate-change/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["These trends suggest two things. Developed countries may be coming to climate negotiations with other concerns at the forefront of their minds. And making significant climate finance commitments to developing states becomes less of ",["a priority","https://www.news24.com.au/politics/australian-politics/australias-climate-commitments-branded-as-hot-air-as-wealthy-nations-slash-finance-pledges-and-countries-hesitate-over-chris-bowens-electrification-target/news-story/5843b101e03992f5171f637bd46512b8#"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Really, APEC?"]},{"k":"p","s":["Finally, there are other global events. The leaders summit at the start of COP31 was timed to avoid a clash with Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation (APEC) meetings. But the abundance of ",["key world leaders at APEC","https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/nov/15/asia-diplomatic-summits-trump-xi-putin-apec-asean"]," compared to COP31 suggests they did not prioritise climate change."]},{"k":"p","s":["Among those at APEC but not at COP31 were China’s Xi Jinping, Russian leader Vladimir Putin, Japan’s prime minister Sanae Takaichi and, of course, US President Donald Trump. Their presence took headlines away from the climate summit at crucial points in the negotiations. Between them, these states account for well over 50% of global ",["greenhouse gas emissions","https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/11/9/which-countries-and-industries-emit-the-most-greenhouse-gases"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":[["China’s announcement","https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/nov/12/china-rules-out-hosting-cop33-climate-summit-in-2028?CMP=Share_iOSApp_Other"]," that it would not seek to host COP33 in 2028 was a blow to lingering hopes that Beijing might step into the leadership vacuum left by the US."]},{"k":"p","s":["At COP31 this week, countries cannot even agree to call out the source of climate change. ",["Every mention of fossil fuels","https://www.theguardian.com/environment/live/2026/nov/19/cop31-climate-turkey-live-news-updates-?CMP=share_btn_url&page=with%3Ablock-6afeeb8c8f087a86897166a8#block-6afeeb8c8f087a86897166a8"]," has been expunged from the draft text for the main outcome of the Cop31 UN climate summit on Thursday, Antalya time, in a blow to the vulnerable countries which had fought hard for its inclusion."]},{"k":"h","s":["What now for climate action?"]},{"k":"p","s":["A ",["recent UN report","https://theconversation.com/a-landmark-un-report-says-the-world-will-overshoot-the-1-5-c-climate-target-what-happens-now-290818"]," confirmed we will likely pass 1.5°C in 2030. That’s bad news for everyone, and clearly points to the ",["imperative for urgent action","https://www.unep.org/en/publications/report/limiting-overshoot-navigating-exceedance-15degc-and-pathways-towards-return"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["We’re running out of time, ",["and overshoot","https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/nov/12/world-heading-for-irrevocable-overshoot-of-global-temperature-thresholds-analysis-warns?CMP=Share_iOSApp_Other"]," means irrevocable sea-level rise and the devastating loss of nature. It will be a disaster for developing countries."]},{"k":"p","s":["Other events are competing for the attention of world leaders, whether traditional security concerns or cost of living crises. Whether COP31 can produce robust agreements that put climate back on the world agenda is the question to be answered over the next two days."]}],"chars":5575},"https://theconversation.com/the-silver-lining-of-the-cop31-flop-climate-coalitions-will-rise-and-the-shift-away-from-fossil-fuels-will-accelerate-297294":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-24T20:53Z","sha":"a421d01b7758539cae1aca7017e37e6686c1da72dd7741ec67a9788fdbae7de4","read":"https://theconversation.com/the-silver-lining-of-the-cop31-flop-climate-coalitions-will-rise-and-the-shift-away-from-fossil-fuels-will-accelerate-297294","bytes":188699,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"The silver lining of the COP31 flop? Climate coalitions will rise – and the shift away from fossil fuels will accelerate","by":[{"n":"Frank Jotzo","r":"Professor, Crawford School of Public Policy and Director, Centre for Climate and Energy Policy, Australian National University"}],"pub":"2026-11-24T19:09:32Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["The failure of the COP31 climate talks to agree on a final text has made a longstanding problem with global agreements on climate policy starkly obvious."]},{"k":"p","s":["That is, in consensus-based multilateral negotiations, some major fossil fuel exporters will work to thwart formal acknowledgment that effective climate action means getting off oil, gas and coal."]},{"k":"p","s":["At COP31, the tensions and divisions over fossil fuels ",["made consensus impossible","https://theconversation.com/how-did-the-cop31-climate-agreement-fall-apart-and-what-happens-now-296994"],". Successive COP talks have now bogged down on this vexed question."]},{"k":"p","s":["This doesn’t mean international climate policy is dead in the water. But it is beginning to take different forms. What we’re likely to see is the growing importance of coalitions of willing, like-minded countries taking action on specific issues."]},{"k":"p","s":["The ",["2015 Paris Agreement","https://unfccc.int/process-and-meetings/the-paris-agreement"]," was the high water mark for globally agreed climate goals, struck when multilateralism was thriving. The institutions and procedures flowing from Paris are now deeply embedded, including national emissions target pledges every five years and the long-term goal of keeping global temperature rise well below 2°C."]},{"k":"p","s":["As the chief executive of last year’s COP30 talks in Brazil, Ana Toni, ",["points out","https://www.linkedin.com/posts/ana-toni_that-cop31-could-not-agree-on-a-cover-decision-share-7530379719062556672-NVXZ/"],", we have “already agreed on much of what we must do, and implementing it does not require consensus”."]},{"k":"h","s":["An unbridgeable chasm"]},{"k":"p","s":["At COP31 in Turkey (also known as Türkiye), it became clear an unbridgeable chasm now exists between countries hooked on fossil fuel revenues and countries deeply concerned about climate change – and who see advantage in ending their energy import dependency by shifting to renewable power."]},{"k":"p","s":["Major fossil fuel producers such as Saudia Arabia and Russia refused to allow any mention of “fossil fuels” in the overall COP31 decision, ",["insisting instead","https://www.reuters.com/sustainability/cop/saudi-arabias-influence-loomed-large-over-failed-cop31-deal-2026-11-23/0"]," on references to “carbon capture and storage”."]},{"k":"p","s":["On the other side, the European Union and many developing countries ",["insisted on","https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cqwy06zrd26lo"]," spelling out the fact that addressing climate change means ending the routine use of fossil fuels."]},{"k":"p","s":["The scientific reality is clear: to achieve climate goals, fossil fuel use will have to ",["fall dramatically","https://www.energy-transitions.org/publications/fossil-fuels-in-transition/"],". Carbon dioxide from most unavoidable fossil fuel use will have to be captured, while major efforts will have to take place to remove carbon dioxide from the atmosphere."]},{"k":"p","s":["It’s better to have no agreed text from these climate talks than one which conceals the need to get away from fossil fuels."]},{"k":"p","s":["This year’s COP flop may well be the reality check – or kick up the backside – which global climate efforts have needed, as some Australian ",["observers and players","https://lnkd.in/p/g_dADZbn"]," have suggested."]},{"k":"h","s":["How did we get here?"]},{"k":"p","s":["It was puzzling the joint COP presidencies, Australia and Turkey, went into the final negotiations with ",["a text","https://docs.unfccc.int/documents/10000420"]," which seemed acceptable to Saudi Arabia and other traditional climate spoilers – but proved unacceptable to the European Union, Pacific nations and other climate vulnerable energy importing countries."]},{"k":"p","s":["Why choose to die on the hill of going slow on climate action, rather than a hill of ambition? Why not take the high ground with a proposed text noting the need for transitioning away from fossil fuels and have it either accepted or publicly shot down by Saudi Arabia and Russia?"]},{"k":"p","s":["The answer is probably that Australia and Turkey believed the EU would agree to the best deal on offer, given it is seen to have such a large stake in the success of the UN climate regime."]},{"k":"p","s":["As it turned out, Europe sent a clear signal: fossil fuels are a red line it will no longer compromise over."]},{"k":"p","s":["The way it played out may lead to international perceptions Australia was somehow complicit in pandering to the fossil fuel lobby. By and large, this is far from the truth. While Australia is a major fossil fuel exporter, it is also focused on its ",["clean energy transition","https://www.dcceew.gov.au/energy/renewable"]," and on ramping up clean energy and mineral exports."]},{"k":"p","s":["Australia’s Climate Change and Energy Minister Chris Bowen was explicit about fossil fuel exports ",["declining","https://www.afr.com/politics/federal/coal-communities-deserve-honesty-about-the-future-bowen-20210210-p57181"]," as far back as 2021. He supported the one and only time a COP decision referred to a transition away from fossil fuels, at ",["COP28","https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2023/dec/13/the-age-of-fossil-fuels-will-end-australias-chris-bowen-hails-cop28-agreement"]," in 2023."]},{"k":"p","s":["Building ",["clean export industries","https://theconversation.com/australia-could-become-the-worlds-first-net-zero-exporter-of-fossil-fuels-heres-how-259037"]," is an avowed national goal, both for economic diversification and to help the global climate effort."]},{"k":"h","s":["Fuelling the rise of clubs and coalitions"]},{"k":"p","s":["The COP result suggests climate action will now increasingly rest on private investment, national policies, ad hoc international collaborations and institutions outside of the consensus-based UN system. Countries with shared interests will increasingly work towards shared goals in coalitions of the willing."]},{"k":"p","s":["One prominent example is the ",["Santa Marta process","https://transitionawayconference.com/"]," on “transitioning away from fossil fuels”. This coalition of like-minded countries – Australia among them – emerged from frustrations at last year’s COP30 talks, when it proved similarly impossible to find agreed language on fossil fuels."]},{"k":"p","s":["The research community is ready to support these efforts, such as through the new ",["Science Panel on the Global Energy Transition","https://www.spget.org/"],". At the first Santa Marta conference this year, many ",["academics and think tanks","https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/at-shadow-climate-summit-on-phasing-out-fossil-fuels-scientists-are-center-stage/"]," volunteered their time to promote practical solutions."]},{"k":"p","s":["Other coalitions are emerging or growing in stature, such as the ",["Climate Club","https://www.climate-club.org/"],", the ",["Integrated Forum on Climate Change and Trade","https://ifcct.org/"]," and the ",["Open Coalition on Compliance Carbon Markets","https://occcm.org/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Similar initiatives could help accelerate development of new carbon dioxide removal technologies and governance, scale up climate finance and adaptation, and coordinate nature and conservation-based approaches."]},{"k":"h","s":["The transition is beginning regardless"]},{"k":"p","s":["COP31 did achieve progress, especially on the “action agenda” running parallel to formal negotiations."]},{"k":"p","s":["Importantly, Australia and Turkey’s flagship “35% by 2035” electrification pledge ",["gained traction","https://theenergy.co/articles/australia-pushes-the-electrification-envelope-at-action-cop31"],", after 75 nations and 180 businesses ",["signed up","https://www.nrdc.org/press-releases/more-75-countries-180-businesses-sign-global-electrification-pledge-cop31"],". Electrification is a win-win, allowing countries to switch to clean, cheap-running ",["electric alternatives","https://theconversation.com/the-world-needs-to-get-serious-about-electrification-heres-why-296062"]," to fossil fuel vehicles, power grids, industrial processes and heating, among others."]},{"k":"p","s":["Momentum is already building as renewables, energy storage, electric vehicles (EVs) and other electric technologies get cheaper and better. Investment in clean energy now accounts for the clear majority of ",["worldwide investment in energy","https://www.iea.org/reports/world-energy-investment-2026"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Global carbon dioxide emissions are set to ",["fall by a fraction in 2026","https://globalcarbonbudget.org/fossil-co2-emissions-decrease-in-2026/"]," due to the steady replacement of fossil fuels with renewables and EVs, galvanised by the Iran war oil supply crisis."]},{"k":"p","s":["What matters in the end isn’t whether the COP text negotiations flopped. Nations are already shifting away from fossil fuels as good, cheap alternatives become available. This is the big picture – and it’s good news."]}],"chars":6537},"https://theconversation.com/weve-documented-almost-1-000-years-lost-to-wrongful-convictions-across-europe-and-were-still-counting-294759":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-27T12:33Z","sha":"2e6193971a21f5cb7a711dc47a3971c2b90a573fdf6b901fac9eecbdbea5d3de","read":"https://theconversation.com/weve-documented-almost-1-000-years-lost-to-wrongful-convictions-across-europe-and-were-still-counting-294759","bytes":211694,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"We’ve documented almost 1,000 years lost to wrongful convictions across Europe – and we’re still counting","by":[{"n":"Jennifer Maria Schell-Leugers","r":"Associate Professor of Legal Psychology, Maastricht University"},{"n":"Linda Marjoleine Geven","r":"Assistant Professor of Criminology, Leiden University"},{"n":"Teresa Schneider","r":"Senior Research Associate, School of Social Work, Lucerne University of Applied Sciences and Arts"}],"pub":"2026-11-27T10:43:39Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["It was a cold October night in 2001 when Rudolf Rupp, a 52-year-old farmer from a small town in Bavaria, Germany, vanished. He had gone out drinking at a local bar, as he often did, and never came home. His wife reported him missing, but no one could find a trace of him or his car. For almost two years, it was as if he had just vanished into thin air. No clues, no body. Just silence."]},{"k":"p","s":["Then the rumours started."]},{"k":"p","s":["Neighbours gossiped that something terrible had happened at the farm – and that Rupp’s family had killed him. The police began to suspect his wife, two teenage daughters, and the boyfriend of one of the girls. All four eventually ",["confessed.","https://rp-online.de/panorama/deutschland/skurriler-prozess-vor-landgericht-ingolstadt_aid-17143101"]]},{"k":"p","s":["The story they told was gruesome: that Rudolf had been ambushed, beaten to death, cut into pieces, and disposed of by feeding him to the farm dogs. The case, especially the grisly details of the confessions, drew ",["nationwide media attention","https://www.nwzonline.de/panorama/vorspann-prozess-bauer-an-die-eigenen-hofhunde-verfuettert_a_6,1,3985632659.html"]," in Germany."]},{"k":"p","s":["But soon after, they ",["took it all back","https://www.spiegel.de/panorama/justiz/getoeteter-bauer-das-raetsel-des-rudolf-rupp-a-724080.html"],", saying they had been pressured by investigators and that their confessions were not true. They all had intellectual disabilities, and they said they were coerced by police."]},{"k":"p","s":["In 2005, despite the fact that no body or any type of evidence was found, ",["they were convicted","https://pfaffenhofen-today.de/22446-rupp11122015"]," based solely on those confessions. Even though they had recanted their confessions and insisted they were innocent."]},{"k":"p","s":["The ",["Insights section","https://theconversation.com/uk/insights"]," is committed to high-quality ",["longform journalism","https://theconversation.com/insights-the-conversations-long-reads-section-240155"],". Our editors work with academics from many different backgrounds who are tackling a wide range of societal and scientific challenges."]},{"k":"p","s":["Then, in 2009, eight years after Rupp vanished, ",["his car was pulled from a river.","https://www.welt.de/print/die_welt/vermischtes/article10681167/Der-Tote-lag-nicht-im-Misthaufen-sondern-in-der-Donau.html"]," His remains were inside, still in one piece, sitting in the driver’s seat. There were no signs that he had been beaten up or cut up. The horrifying story the family had told could not possibly have happened."]},{"k":"p","s":["The case was reopened, and in a full retrial before the Landshut Regional Court the family was ",["acquitted in 2011","https://www.spiegel.de/panorama/justiz/getoeteter-landwirt-gericht-spricht-im-fall-rupp-familie-frei-a-747654.html"],". They spent several years in prison for a crime they did not commit, a story they had confessed to under pressure."]},{"k":"p","s":["No one ever formally investigated how the family’s specific confessions were extracted. The family’s defence had long claimed coercion, and over time some corroborating evidence emerged."]},{"k":"p","s":["Police had filmed themselves having the suspects “reconstruct” the crime at the farm, and a criminal law professor who later reviewed that footage described the officers’ approach as inquisitorial and leading, ",["saying","https://www.drburkhard.de/rudi-rupp-lebt-ueberall-unter-uns-auch-im-steuerrecht-und-steuerstrafrecht/"]," suspects simply could not be questioned that way."]},{"k":"p","s":["Separately, a scrap dealer whom police suspected of disposing of Rupp’s car ",["testified","https://www.sueddeutsche.de/bayern/methoden-der-polizei-absonderlichkeiten-aus-der-verhoerstube-1.1554051"]," at the retrial that, when he would not give the statement investigators wanted, an officer from the same police unit held a service weapon to his head. No officer was investigated. Instead, prosecutors put the scrap dealer on trial for making a false accusation. In December 2012, the court acquitted him."]},{"k":"p","s":["All four family members were described by their defence lawyer as having cognitive vulnerabilities, which made them especially susceptible to coercive questioning. And although it became clear the family had confessed to something they did not do, they were never compensated, because the retrial court held them partly responsible for their own false statements."]},{"k":"p","s":["Sadly, the Rupp case is far from unique."]},{"k":"h","s":["Wrongful convictions and the EUREX database"]},{"k":"p","s":["When Ina Post addressed the audience at the ",["European Innocence Network","https://bassoon-buffalo-p3s5.squarespace.com/"]," meeting in Amsterdam in 2019, the room fell silent. Her presence was calm, but the weight of her story was overwhelming."]},{"k":"p","s":["In 1986, she was wrongfully convicted in the Netherlands of murdering an elderly woman she cared for. The only evidence was a confession. A confession she gave after hours of intense, suggestive police interrogation while in a psychologically vulnerable state. There was no forensic proof, and much of what she had said turned out to be factually incorrect. Still, it took more than two decades of legal struggle before she was finally ",["acquitted in 2010","https://englishdocs.eu/ina-post-wrongly-convicted-without-evidence/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Listening to her that day was deeply moving. The room was silent – you could have heard a pin drop. Her story was not just about what had happened to her, it was about how easily justice can go wrong, and how hard it is to make it right again. Her words stayed with us."]},{"k":"p","s":["As researchers in the field of false confessions and wrongful convictions, we asked ourselves: how many others have suffered similar fates? We knew that the United States tracked exoneration cases through the ",["National Registry of Exonerations","https://exonerationregistry.org/"],", but Europe had no central database, no coordinated effort to track wrongful convictions. There were no numbers, no overview of the problem and no one was trying to change that."]},{"k":"p","s":["That is why we created ",["EUREX","https://www.registryofexonerations.eu/"]," – the European Registry of Exonerations. Its mission is to archive and analyse exonerations across Europe. EUREX is a centralised database that collects and publishes information about cases where individuals were wrongfully convicted and later cleared of all charges."]},{"k":"p","s":["Beyond documenting these cases, EUREX seeks to understand their causes and consequences, identify systemic patterns, and ultimately contribute to improving legal decision-making. In the long run, we aim to lead empirical research that tests these patterns and informs reforms to prevent future miscarriages of justice."]},{"k":"p","s":["Wrongful convictions are often thought of as rare and tragic mistakes. But the reality is more complex."]},{"k":"p","s":["So far, ",["we have documented","https://www.registryofexonerations.eu/"]," 150 cases in 20 countries since 1981 in which people were officially declared innocent after years – sometimes decades – behind bars."]},{"k":"p","s":["Together, these individuals lost more than 970 years of freedom. Our first ",["systematic analysis","https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2589871X26000537?via%3Dihub"]," of the registry (covering the first 144 cases and published in 2026 in Forensic Science International: Synergy) showed that most had been convicted of very serious crimes such as murder or sexual assault. This highlights the devastating consequences when errors occur in high-stakes trials."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more: ",["‘People think you come out … and live happily ever after. If only.’ The reality of life after wrongful conviction","https://theconversation.com/people-think-you-come-out-and-live-happily-ever-after-if-only-the-reality-of-life-after-wrongful-conviction-257060"]]},{"k":"p","s":["These numbers are exactly why a project like EUREX matters: at its ",["launch","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=swhap1Gyr0A&feature=youtu.be&cbrd=1"],", Saul Kassin, a social psychologist who specialises in false confessions, described the registry as filling a real gap in the field, one that could give researchers, legal practitioners, and policymakers across Europe a shared evidence base for understanding how wrongful convictions happen – something that has been missing until now."]},{"k":"p","s":["Innocence lawyer, Geert-Jan Knoops, speaking at the same ",["event","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_X5pIwWXfHQ"],", put it more bluntly: “With EUREX, we now have objective proof that we are not dealing with isolated incidents.”"]},{"k":"h","s":["False confessions"]},{"k":"p","s":["Across the world, false confessions have played a significant role in wrongful convictions. For example, about 35% of the cases in ",["EUREX","https://www.registryofexonerations.eu/"]," involve someone who confessed to a crime they did not commit."]},{"k":"p","s":["Research shows that often ",["false confessions","https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2021.633936/full"]," happen to the most vulnerable: teenagers, people with intellectual disabilities, and those with mental health issues. Under pressure in a police interrogation, they may say what the interrogators want to hear – just to get out of the room. In many cases, the combination of personal vulnerabilities and stressful surroundings creates a powerful mix of factors that can lead to a false confession."]},{"k":"p","s":[["Scientists","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39928489/"]," have identified three types of false confessions: voluntary, coerced-compliant and coerced-internalised. A voluntary false confession happens when someone steps forward without any pressure, often to protect someone else, gain attention, or because they mistakenly believe confessing will benefit them. Coerced-compliant confessions occur when a suspect knows they are innocent but gives in simply to end the interrogation or secure what seems like a better deal. Finally, coerced-internalised confessions are the most disturbing: after intense questioning, a person may start doubting their own memory and come to believe they committed the crime."]},{"k":"p","s":["The kind that shows up most often in courts is the coerced one. These confessions typically follow hours of questioning, psychological manipulation, and physical or emotional exhaustion. Suspects may be deprived of sleep, denied breaks, or led to believe that confessing is the only way out. Without a lawyer present, vulnerable people are especially likely to agree to whatever investigators suggest."]},{"k":"p","s":["False confession expert ",["Gisli Gudjonsson","https://www.frontiersin.org/news/2021/02/22/frontiers-psychology-causes-false-confessions-memory-distrust-syndrome-crime-forensic-science"],", writing in a Frontiers in Psychology editorial summarising his own 40-year review of the science, explained: “Until detailed analysis of real-life cases, peer-reviewed research, and DNA exonerations began to emerge in the 1980s and 1990s, it was widely believed that only those with intellectual disabilities or mental illness were susceptible to giving false confessions. This ‘common sense’ view is a fallacy. False confessions are caused by a dynamic process and a combination of ‘risk factors’, which is not confined to the mentally impaired. In fact, most of those who give a coerced confession to police are not mentally impaired.”"]},{"k":"p","s":["Once a confession is made, even if it is false and there is no other evidence, it can shape how everyone sees the case, from law enforcement to judges. It becomes hard to undo. Generally, people assume that no one would confess to something they did not do, so distinguishing false confessions from genuine ones is extremely challenging."]},{"k":"p","s":["That is why the story of the Rupp family should serve as a warning about what can go wrong when confessions are treated as proof, even when there is no other evidence."]},{"k":"h","s":["How widespread is the problem?"]},{"k":"p","s":["And yet, these numbers only tell part of the story. EUREX uses a deliberately strict definition: it only includes cases in which someone was convicted by final judgment and later officially cleared after the case was reopened, through an acquittal or a formal declaration of innocence. Countless other cases never clear that bar. Doubts may linger for years, new evidence may surface too late, or legal hurdles, procedural limits and institutional resistance may close the door before a case can be reopened at all. The real number of wrongful convictions is almost certainly higher than any registry can capture."]},{"k":"p","s":["It is hard to say whether wrongful convictions themselves are becoming more or less frequent. What does seem to be rising is our ability and willingness to catch them. More than half of the exonerations we found happened after 2010, which probably reflects growing efforts to revisit old cases, improve fair investigative practices, and develop more transparent review processes."]},{"k":"p","s":["One thing the registry makes clear is that wrongful convictions can happen to almost anyone. Exonerees come from different countries, income levels, professions, and ethnic backgrounds. Most are men, but this mirrors the prison population as a whole: men account for the vast majority of people convicted of serious crimes such as murder and sexual assault."]},{"k":"p","s":["As ",["Saul Kassin","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=swhap1Gyr0A&feature=youtu.be&cbrd=1"],", who has spent decades studying wrongful convictions, said in an interview with us:"]},{"k":"p","s":["These are not all the same types of people. This is not about who they are. There are men and women, adults and children, people speaking different languages, from different countries and with different skin colours. Exonerations aren’t limited to a certain type of person – it is a systemic problem."]},{"k":"p","s":["Wrongful convictions aren’t explained by who the exonerated individuals are, but by what they lose. On average, the exonerees we found were about 36 years old at the time of their conviction – an age when most people are building careers, starting families, and laying plans for the future. Losing those years leaves scars that are personal, social, and financial all at once."]},{"k":"p","s":["In subsequent interviews, ",["Ina Post","https://eur03.safelinks.protection.outlook.com/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.ad.nl%2Fbinnenland%2Fvrijgesproken-maar-wat-geluk-is-weet-ik-niet-meer%7Ea1e4d0da%2F&data=05%7C02%7Cl.m.geven%40law.leidenuniv.nl%7C85aa39f3d71d43cb6fde08de33e2e110%7Cca2a7f76dbd74ec091086b3d524fb7c8%7C0%7C0%7C639005246689659570%7CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1hcGkiOnRydWUsIlYiOiIwLjAuMDAwMCIsIlAiOiJXaW4zMiIsIkFOIjoiTWFpbCIsIldUIjoyfQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&sdata=gdvfh1YG1WBaYl1Bsqy7uoP2rcVEFcpU9GPeYoqU9Yk%3D&reserved=0"]," has described what that loss feels like: “I was 30 years old when I was arrested. I was married. My husband and I wanted children. While I was in detention, I decided to get a divorce. When you are imprisoned, you rob your partner of their life, too.”"]},{"k":"p","s":["Wrongful convictions are better understood not as a matter of who is convicted, but of how convictions come about. The patterns identified by these cases reflect precisely this: recurring flaws in the processes that produce them."]},{"k":"p","s":["False confessions, like the ones at the centre of the Rupp and Post cases, appear in 35% of cases we have examined. Flawed forensic evidence played a role in 24%, a reminder that scientific-sounding evidence is only as reliable as the people collecting, evaluating and interpreting it. Eyewitness misidentification (23%) and false accusations (35%) round out the list, underlining just how fallible human memory and perception can be under pressure."]},{"k":"p","s":["One of the registry’s most striking findings concerns what happens to the actual perpetrator. In 91 cases, an innocent person was convicted for a crime committed by someone else, and in roughly 40% of those, the real culprit was never identified at all."]},{"k":"p","s":["That means the system failed twice: once by punishing the wrong person, and again by leaving the guilty one free. As Kassin warns: “When you close a case on an innocent person, you leave the offender free to reoffend – and they often do.”"]},{"k":"p","s":["The scale of this risk is stark: ",["a study","https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1111/1745-9133.12463?casa_token=PwtN8Ih_H9IAAAAA%3AnHjzTXewrfMv3MMDqjnkswrB6uFiWOyoO9cBZCakEU_D34MimHPoAhoasfZgSYY4s_KI4HVJlk9jBnoMDA"]," of 109 real perpetrators later identified in the United States found that 94% went on to commit further crimes, including murder, while innocent people sat behind bars in their place. Failing to catch the true offender does not just deny justice to the wrongfully convicted; it puts the public in danger."]},{"k":"h","s":["‘My nightmare is over’ – Tomasz Komenda"]},{"k":"p","s":["On New Year’s Eve 1996, 15-year-old Małgorzata Kwiatkowska vanished after a party in Miłoszyce, a small village in south-western Poland. What started as a festive New Year’s Eve ended tragically when ",["her dead body was discovered","https://kronikidziejow.pl/kryminalne/zbrodnia-miloszycka-smierc-zlamane-zycie-i-sprawiedliwosc-na-ktora-trzeba-bylo-dlugo-czekac/"]," in the snow the next morning. She had been raped and left to die of internal injuries and hypothermia. The crime scene revealed multiple DNA profiles, bite marks, and other forensic clues – yet for years, the case went cold."]},{"k":"p","s":["Then, in 2000, a TV programme about the unsolved murder aired a composite sketch of a man seen with Małgorzata on the night she disappeared. A viewer tipped off police, claiming it resembled Tomasz Komenda. Despite thin physical evidence and an alibi that held up, Komenda was arrested, charged, and while insisting he was innocent, ",["convicted in 2003","https://www.gov.pl/web/prokuratura-krajowa/sad-najwyzszy-uniewinnil-tomasza-komende-od-zbrodni-zabojstw"],". He was sentenced to 15 years, later extended to 25 years on appeal."]},{"k":"p","s":["Because Komenda refused to admit guilt, he was ineligible for parole. Over nearly two decades in prison, he endured physical abuse, isolation, and psychological torment, and attempted suicide more than once. “I thought I would never get out,” he later told ",["TVN24","https://tvn24.pl/polska/tomasz-komenda-myslalem-ze-mnie-zabija-w-kryminale-ra822545-ls2022872"],". “I thought they would kill me in prison”."]},{"k":"p","s":["The case ",["cracked open in 2016","https://uwaga.tvn.pl/reportaze/remigiusz-korejwo-kim-jest-policjant-ktory-pomogl-tomkowi-komendzie-ls6641626"],", when a police officer reviewing old cases spotted major flaws in the original investigation. New DNA testing excluded Komenda entirely and instead pointed to two other men."]},{"k":"p","s":["Komenda walked free on March 15, 2018, after 18 years in prison. That May, the Polish Supreme Court ",["formally overturned his conviction","https://www.gov.pl/web/prokuratura-krajowa/sad-najwyzszy-uniewinnil-tomasza-komende-od-zbrod"]," and declared him innocent. Standing on the courtroom steps after spending 6,540 days behind bars for a crime he did not commit, Komenda told reporters: “ ",["My nightmare is over","https://wydarzenia.interia.pl/kraj/news-tomasz-komenda-po-wyroku-dzis-skonczyl-sie-moj-koszmar,nId,5037225"],"”."]},{"k":"p","s":["But freedom did not erase what prison had done to him. In ",["a television interview","https://tvn24.pl/polska/superwizjer-sprawa-tomasza-komendy-ra870299-ls2397685"]," Komenda described how fear and anxiety followed him into daily life. “Even now, I get scared when I hear a loud noise. I still dream about the prison”."]},{"k":"p","s":[["In 2021","https://wiadomosci.onet.pl/kraj/wyrok-ws-odszkodowania-dla-tomasza-komendy-komenda-skonczyl-sie-moj-koszmar/89xl7z1"],", he was awarded nearly 13 million zloty (about €2.75 million) in compensation for the years he had lost. But, as he and many other exonerees have said, no amount of money can make up for the lost years."]},{"k":"p","s":["Psychological research supports what Komenda described. A ",["landmark 2004 study","https://psycnet.apa.org/record/2004-10288-004"]," found that wrongfully convicted people often develop post-traumatic stress disorder, depression, and other lasting emotional wounds. In some cases it is severe enough to resemble the aftermath of torture."]},{"k":"p","s":["A later ",["systematic review","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32807017/"]," reached a similar conclusion, observing that the psychological toll of being wrongfully accused is often severe and long-lasting, and that it can quietly erode someone’s sense of identity, health, relationships, finances, and trust in institutions, while adding a lingering ",["social stigma","https://psycnet.apa.org/record/2018-37845-004"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["That stigma tends to outlast the conviction itself. Even after exoneration, many people struggle to find work, secure housing, and rebuild a social life. They are often without any of the structured support that is typically offered to parolees. So in practice, being cleared of a crime does not always mean the sentence is over. For many exonerees, it just becomes a different kind of life sentence."]},{"k":"p","s":["For ",["Komenda","https://www.registryofexonerations.eu/case_details/tomasz-komenda-attempted-murdermanslaughter-2003/"],", that second sentence was cruelly brief. In May 2024, he died of cancer at just 46 years old. His case prompted a national reckoning in Poland over police misconduct and flawed forensic work, and it pushed the question of how Europe treats exonerees onto the wider public agenda."]},{"k":"p","s":["Wrongful convictions are not just legal failures. They are human ones. Komenda’s story is a painful reminder of the damage they do and of how long the road to healing can be, even after freedom is won."]},{"k":"h","s":["Addressing the problem"]},{"k":"p","s":["Across Europe, reform has often come in the wake of scandal. In the Netherlands, the ",["Schiedammer Park murder case","https://www.ditistwee.nl/nieuws/22801/25-jaar-na-de-schiedammer-parkmoord-een-van-de-grootste-gerechtelijke-dwalingen-van-nederland"]," exposed serious flaws in investigative practices and prosecutorial oversight. Public outrage led the College of Procurators General to set up the ",["Commissie-Posthumus","https://zoek.officielebekendmakingen.nl/kst-29800-VI-168-b1.pdf"]," in 2005."]},{"k":"p","s":["Its landmark report identified real vulnerabilities in how criminal cases were built and recommended concrete fixes; among them, bringing in independent forensic advisors and building explicit safeguards against “tunnel vision” – the tendency for investigators to fixate on one suspect and stop questioning their own theory."]},{"k":"p","s":["These recommendations also led to the creation of the ",["Adviescommissie Afgesloten Strafzaken","https://www.hogeraad.nl/bijzondere-taken/herziening/"]," (ACAS) in 2012, an advisory body that can recommend cases for reopening when new evidence casts doubt on a conviction."]},{"k":"p","s":["Norway has gone further still, establishing a ",["Criminal Cases Review Commission","https://www.gjenopptakelse.no/en/"]," with full statutory powers to reopen convictions whenever significant new evidence emerges (unlike the Dutch ACAS, which can only advise the Prosecutor General and does not have the power to reopen cases itself)."]},{"k":"p","s":["By December 2020, the Norwegian commission had reopened 351 cases. One of its most striking successes is the case of ",["Viggo Kristiansen","https://www.newsinenglish.no/2021/06/02/murder-convict-released-after-21-years/"],", convicted in 2001 (and again on appeal in 2002) of raping and murdering two young girls in Kristiansand, and given Norway’s maximum sentence at the time: 21 years. The case against him rested on a co-defendant’s testimony, weak DNA traces, and a psychological profile. Kristiansen spent nearly two decades insisting he was innocent and filing appeals that went nowhere."]},{"k":"p","s":["Then in 2021, the commission agreed to reopen the case: new DNA testing excluded him, and witness statements that had previously been dismissed turned out to support his alibi after all. In December 2022, the Borgarting Court of Appeal ",["acquitted him outright","https://www.nrk.no/norge/viggo-kristiansen-frikjent-i-baneheia-saken-1.16220368"],", concluding his co-defendant had acted alone. Kristiansen later received substantial compensation. His case makes the argument for independent review bodies: they give a wrongful conviction a real, structured path back to court."]},{"k":"p","s":["In the United Kingdom, concerns about miscarriages of justice led to the creation of a dedicated review body in 1997 – ",["the Criminal Cases Review Commission","https://ccrc.gov.uk/how-it-all-began/"],". But the watchdog has been plunged into crisis and faced ",["relentless criticism","https://theconversation.com/the-miscarriage-of-justice-watchdog-is-failing-at-its-only-job-heres-how-to-fix-it-247623"]," about the way it has handled recent cases."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more: ",["The miscarriage of justice watchdog is failing at its only job – here’s how to fix it","https://theconversation.com/the-miscarriage-of-justice-watchdog-is-failing-at-its-only-job-heres-how-to-fix-it-247623"]]},{"k":"p","s":["But such safeguards are far from universal. For example, Belgium and Portugal record zero exonerations in the registry, almost certainly not because their justice systems get it right every time, but because structural barriers make miscarriages of justice harder to surface or overturn in the first place. Narrow rules for reopening closed cases, no independent review commission, and limited legal aid after conviction all add up to a system where mistakes can simply stay buried."]},{"k":"p","s":["At the European level, the gap is even wider. Criminal justice is still handled almost entirely at the national level, and there’s no EU-wide framework for catching or correcting wrongful convictions. Human rights law offers broad principles, but not the practical tools needed to identify and fix specific errors."]},{"k":"p","s":["“One does not easily get past 24 lost years,” ",["said Post","https://www.rtl.nl/nederland/artikel/2768616/ina-post-na-eerherstel-nog-altijd-niet-gelukkig"],", who spent four years in prison and more than two decades fighting to finally clear her name."]},{"k":"p","s":["Wrongful convictions will never be eliminated entirely, but they can be made rarer, easier to detect, and faster to repair. Independent review commissions, safeguards against coercive interrogations, and robust forensic standards are not abstract ideals – they are practical steps grounded in evidence."]},{"k":"p","s":["Building these reforms into national systems and pooling what is learned across borders is how Europe makes sure that justice does not fail the very people it is meant to protect."]},{"k":"p","s":["For you: more from our ",["Insights series","https://theconversation.com/uk/topics/insights-series-71218?utm_source=TCUK&utm_medium=linkback&utm_campaign=TCUKengagement&utm_content=InsightsUK"],":"]},{"k":"li","s":[["Kyrgyzstan’s Dordoi Bazaar: a world made from shipping containers and a city within a city","https://theconversation.com/kyrgyzstans-dordoi-bazaar-a-world-made-from-shipping-containers-and-a-city-within-a-city-287083"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["Britain’s quarter century: wealth and widening inequality","https://theconversation.com/britains-quarter-century-wealth-and-widening-inequality-287410"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["Free will: does refusing to believe in it make you a ‘bad’ person? Research is challenging this old idea","https://theconversation.com/free-will-does-refusing-to-believe-in-it-make-you-a-bad-person-research-is-challenging-this-old-idea-283307"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["Inside Porton Down: what I learned during three years at the UK’s most secretive chemical weapons laboratory","https://theconversation.com/inside-porton-down-what-i-learned-during-three-years-at-the-uks-most-secretive-chemical-weapons-laboratory-248376"]]},{"k":"p","s":["To hear about new Insights articles, join the hundreds of thousands of people who value The Conversation’s evidence-based news. ",["Subscribe to our newsletter","https://theconversation.com/uk/newsletters/the-daily-newsletter-2?utm_source=TCUK&utm_medium=linkback&utm_campaign=TCUKengagement&utm_content=InsightsUK"],"."]}],"chars":21624}};
